var e=JSON.parse('[[1001,"`MessageChannel` is an API in JavaScript that lets you create a channel for exchanging messages between threads, such as the main thread and Web Workers. It consists of two objects, `port1` and `port2`, which can be used to send and receive messages. This mechanism provides two-way communication and helps synchronise the actions between threads.","`MessageChannel` is a built-in object in JavaScript that lets you create a channel for exchanging messages between different threads. That is especially useful for the interaction between the main thread and Web Workers.\\nWhen you create an instance of `MessageChannel` you obtain two ports: `port1` and `port2`, each of which can be used to send and receive messages.\\nAn example of the use of `MessageChannel`:\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- The creation of the channel: First a new instance of `MessageChannel` is created.\\n\\n- The setting up of the handlers: Message handlers are set for a port, in order to handle the data received.\\n\\n- The sending of messages: By means of the `postMessage` method you can send messages between the two ports.\\n\\n`MessageChannel` thus provides two-way communication, letting threads exchange data in real time, which is useful for synchronising the work between different parts of an application.","// The creation of a new channel \\nconst channel = new MessageChannel(); \\n// Obtaining the references to the ports \\nconst port1 = channel.port1; \\nconst port2 = channel.port2; \\n// The handler for receiving messages on port1 \\nport1.onmessage = function(event) {\\n  console.log(\'Received from port2:\', event.data); \\n}; \\n// The sending of a message from port2 \\nport2.postMessage(\'Hello from port2!\'); \\n// The sending of a message from port1 \\nport1.postMessage(\'Hello from port1!\');"],[1002,"In the browser several mechanisms for holding data are available: `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, `IndexedDB` and `Cookies`. `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` are intended for holding key-value pairs, but `localStorage` preserves the data for an unlimited time, whereas `sessionStorage` is cleared when the tab is closed. `IndexedDB` is a more complex store for holding significant volumes of structured data, while `Cookies` are used to hold small volumes of data and to pass them to the server with every request.","In browsers there are several methods of holding data, each of which has its own peculiarities and application:\\nlocalStorage:\\n\\n- Holds data in the key-value format and is available to every tab and window of the same origin.\\n\\n- The data is preserved even after the tab or the browser has been closed and is available until the user deletes it by hand or through code.\\n\\n- The maximum size of the data is usually about 5-10 MB.\\n\\nsessionStorage:\\n\\n- Similar to `localStorage`, but the data is held only within the current session. That means the data is available only in the current tab and will be deleted when it is closed.\\n\\n- The size of the data is also about 5-10 MB.\\n\\nIndexedDB:\\n\\n- A more complex, powerful store for holding large volumes of structured data. It supports transactions, indexes and queries.\\n\\n- It suits complex web applications requiring the holding of large volumes of data.\\n\\n- It works asynchronously, which prevents the interface from being blocked.\\n\\nCookies:\\n\\n- Used to hold small volumes of data that can be sent to the server with every HTTP request. They have a period of validity, which can be set.\\n\\n- Most often applied to hold information about the user\'s session and about authentication.\\n\\n- The maximum size is about 4 KB per cookie.\\n\\nA comparison:\\n\\n- localStorage and sessionStorage are ideally suited to the simple holding of data.\\n\\n- IndexedDB is necessary for working with large volumes of structured data.\\n\\n- Cookies are used to pass information between the client and the server, but they have restrictions as regards size.\\n\\nAn understanding of these different methods of holding data will help developers manage users\' data effectively in web applications.","localStorage.setItem(\'key\', \'value\'); \\nconst value = localStorage.getItem(\'key\');\\n\\nsessionStorage.setItem(\'key\', \'value\'); \\nconst value = sessionStorage.getItem(\'key\');\\n\\nconst request = indexedDB.open(\'myDatabase\', 1); \\nrequest.onsuccess = function(event) {\\n  const db = event.target.result;    \\n  // Further operations with the database \\n};\\n\\ndocument.cookie = \\"key=value; expires=Fri, 31 Dec 9999 23:59:59 GMT\\";"],[1003,"The caching of data lets you improve the performance of web applications substantially. The main strategies of caching include caching on the client\'s side, where the data is held in the user\'s browser (using `localStorage` or `IndexedDB`, for example), and caching on the server\'s side, where the data is saved on the server for reuse.\\nIt is also worth taking caching at the level of the network into account — the use of a CDN (Content Delivery Network), for example, which lets you hold and transfer static resources quickly. The correct use of caching strategies helps shorten the loading time and improve the user experience.","The caching of data is an important part of developing web applications, since it helps speed up access to information and reduce the load on the server. There are several strategies of caching:\\nCaching on the client\'s side:\\n\\n- localStorage and sessionStorage: These APIs let you hold data on the client\'s side. `localStorage` holds the data even after the tab has been closed, while `sessionStorage` holds it only until the current session is closed. That is especially useful for caching the user\'s settings or temporary data.\\n\\n- IndexedDB: This is an asynchronous store, letting you hold large volumes of structured data. It suits complex applications where the fast, effective obtaining of data is necessary.\\n\\nCaching on the server\'s side:\\n\\n- The holding of frequently requested data in memory (using Redis, for example) lets you substantially reduce the response time to requests. That helps you avoid turning to the database repeatedly to obtain the same information.\\n\\n- The use of HTTP headers such as `Cache-Control` and `ETag` lets browsers cache the responses to requests, thereby reducing the number of requests to the server.\\n\\nCaching at the level of the network:\\n\\n- The use of a CDN (Content Delivery Network) lets you hold static resources (images, CSS, JavaScript) on several servers around the world. That shortens the loading time, since the user receives the data from the nearest server.\\n\\nMixed strategies:\\n\\n- The combination of client-side and server-side caching lets you create a more flexible architecture. You can cache the result of an API request on the client\'s side and use server-side caching for the more frequent requests, for example.\\n\\nThe application of these strategies lets you not only improve the performance of web applications but also create a more responsive, comfortable user experience.","localStorage.setItem(\'userSettings\', JSON.stringify(settings)); \\nconst settings = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\'userSettings\'));"],[1004,"The optimisation of work with the DOM includes minimising the number of manipulations of elements, reducing the number of repaints and using a virtual DOM where that is possible.\\nThe best practices include the grouping of operations on the DOM, the use of a `documentFragment` for the temporary holding of changes and the avoidance of synchronous access to elements in loops. It also matters to use CSS for animation instead of JavaScript, in order to reduce the load on the processor and raise the smoothness of the interface\'s working.","Work with the DOM (Document Object Model) in web applications can be expensive in time, especially if elements on the page have to be changed often. The optimisation of work with the DOM and the minimisation of the rendering can substantially improve an application\'s performance. Here are several effective methods:\\nMinimising the manipulations of the DOM:\\n\\n- Try to limit the number of changes you bring into the DOM. Every change can cause the page to be repainted, which slows the application\'s working down.\\n\\n- Instead of carrying out several separate operations of updating, group them. First create every element in memory, for example, and then add them to the DOM.\\n\\nThe use of a `documentFragment`:\\n\\n- A `documentFragment` lets you temporarily hold changes that do not affect the display until it is added to the DOM. That reduces the number of repaints.\\n\\nAvoiding frequent access to the DOM:\\n\\n- Measuring and accessing DOM elements, especially in loops, can be slow. Save the references to the elements in variables and reuse them.\\n\\nThe use of CSS for animation:\\n\\n- Animations implemented by means of CSS usually work faster than those created by means of JavaScript. That is connected with the fact that browsers optimise CSS animations and can use hardware acceleration.\\n\\nThe virtual DOM:\\n\\n- If you use libraries such as React, the virtual DOM helps minimise the number of operations on the real DOM by comparing the changes and only then updating it.\\n\\nThese methods let you optimise the interaction with the DOM, improving an application\'s overall performance and ensuring a smoother, more responsive user interface.","const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment(); \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n  const newElement = document.createElement(\'div\');    \\n  newElement.textContent = `Item ${i}`;    \\n  fragment.appendChild(newElement); \\n} \\ndocument.body.appendChild(fragment);\\n\\nconst element = document.getElementById(\'myElement\'); \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n  element.textContent = `Updated text ${i}`; \\n}"],[1005,"The widespread vulnerabilities in JavaScript applications include XSS (cross-site scripting), CSRF (cross-site request forgery) and SQL injections. To prevent XSS it matters to clean and escape user input, and to protect against CSRF, to use tokens and headers. Besides that, parameters should be applied for queries to a database, in order to avoid SQL injections. An understanding and the application of these security measures help protect applications from attacks.","The security of JavaScript applications is an important part of their development, since widespread vulnerabilities can lead to the leak of data or to the abuse of an application\'s functionality.\\nHere are some of the most widespread vulnerabilities and the ways of preventing them:\\n\\n- XSS (cross-site scripting):\\nXSS attacks happen when an attacker inserts malicious JavaScript code into a web page. That can happen if the user\'s input is not cleaned properly before being displayed on the page.\\nHow to prevent it: Use escaping functions to clean the data before printing it to the page. It is also worth applying a CSP (Content Security Policy), which helps restrict the execution of scripts.\\n\\n- CSRF (cross-site request forgery):\\nCSRF attacks happen when an attacker forces a user to carry out an undesirable action on a web site where they are authenticated.\\nHow to prevent it: Use unique tokens, which have to be included in every request that changes state (POST, PUT, DELETE). Also apply headers such as `SameSite` for the cookies.\\n\\n- SQL injections:\\nSQL injections arise when an attacker inserts malicious SQL code into a query to a database through user input.\\nHow to prevent it: Use prepared statements or an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) in order to avoid injections. Always check and clean user input.\\n\\n- Errors of authentication and session management:\\nVulnerabilities in the mechanisms of authentication can lead to unauthorised access to users\' data.\\nHow to prevent it: Use secure methods for holding passwords (bcrypt, for example) and implement multi-factor authentication.\\n\\nAn understanding of these vulnerabilities and the application of the best practices of security help protect your JavaScript applications from attacks, keeping users\' data safe and ensuring the reliable working of the application.","// An example of escaping \\nfunction escapeHtml(unsafe) {\\n  return unsafe        \\n    .replace(/&/g, \\"&amp;\\")        \\n    .replace(/</g, \\"&lt;\\")        \\n    .replace(/>/g, \\"&gt;\\")        \\n    .replace(/\\"/g, \\"&quot;\\")        \\n    .replace(/\'/g, \\"&#039;\\"); \\n}\\n\\n// An example of the use of a prepared statement \\nconst query = \'SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?\'; \\ndb.query(query, [username], (err, results) => {\\n  // the handling of the results \\n});"],[1006,"A Content Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that lets developers control which resources can be loaded and carried out on a web page. A CSP is implemented by means of the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP header, where you can state the permitted sources of scripts, styles and other resources. That helps prevent the loading of malicious content and reduces the risk of XSS-type attacks.\\nTo introduce a CSP it is enough to add the corresponding header on the server or in a meta tag on the page.","A Content Security Policy (CSP) is a powerful tool for protecting web applications from attacks such as cross-site scripting (XSS). A CSP lets developers set rules that determine where various resources (scripts, styles, images and so on) can be loaded from, thereby restricting the potential threats.\\nHow does it work? A CSP is implemented by means of the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP header or through a meta tag in the HTML document. This header tells the browser which sources of content are permitted. You can permit the execution of scripts only from your domain and block every other source, for example.\\nAn example of a CSP HTTP header:\\n\\nThis header permits the loading of resources only from the current domain (`\'self\'`), the scripts can also be loaded from `https://trustedscripts.example.com`, and the images can be loaded from the current domain and from `data:` URIs.\\nExamples of use: To apply a CSP, add the corresponding header to the settings of your web server. In Apache, for example, that can be done by adding the following line to the configuration file:\\n\\nThe advantages of using a CSP: A CSP helps prevent the loading and execution of malicious code, which substantially reduces the risk of successful XSS attacks. Besides that, a CSP can serve as an additional level of protection for applications that already use other security measures.\\nRecommendations:\\n\\n- Begin with stricter rules and gradually add exceptions.\\n\\n- Use CSP analysis tools in order to discover problems and track potential threats.\\n\\n- Switch on the `report-uri` or `report-to` mode in order to receive reports of violations of the CSP.\\n\\nThe implementation of a Content Security Policy is thus an important step towards raising the security of your web applications and protecting users\' data.","Content-Security-Policy: default-src \'self\'; \\nscript-src \'self\' https://trustedscripts.example.com; \\nimg-src \'self\' data:;\\n\\nHeader set Content-Security-Policy \\"default-src \'self\'; \\nscript-src \'self\' https://trustedscripts.example.com;\\""],[1007,"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is an attack in which an attacker inserts a malicious script into a web page, which lets them gain access to the user\'s data.\\nProtection against XSS includes the escaping of user input and the application of a Content Security Policy (CSP).\\nCross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is an attack in which an attacker forces a user to carry out an undesirable action on a site where they are authenticated.\\nProtection against CSRF includes the use of unique tokens to confirm requests and the checking of headers.","XSS and CSRF are widespread vulnerabilities that can seriously damage the security of web applications.\\nLet us look at each of them and at the methods of protection:\\n\\n- Cross-Site Scripting (XSS):\\n\\n- XSS attacks happen when an attacker inserts malicious JavaScript code into a web page, which is then carried out in other users\' browsers. That can lead to the theft of data, of sessions and much else.\\n\\nProtection against XSS:\\n\\n- The escaping of user input: All of the data received from users has to be escaped before being displayed on the page. That prevents the execution of malicious code.\\n\\n- The use of a Content Security Policy (CSP): A CSP lets you restrict the sources of content, which reduces the likelihood of malicious scripts being carried out.\\n\\n- The validation and filtering of input: Check and filter user input in order to prevent the insertion of malicious code.\\n\\n- Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF):\\nCSRF attacks happen when an attacker forces an authenticated user to carry out an undesirable action on a web site. That can be a change of the account\'s settings or the sending of a payment, for example.\\nProtection against CSRF:\\n\\n- The use of tokens: Generate unique tokens for every request that requires a change of state. The token has to be checked on the server, in order to be sure the request was initiated by the user.\\n\\n- The checking of headers: Check the headers of the request, such as `Referer` and `Origin`, in order to be sure the request came from a trusted source.\\n\\nAn understanding and the implementation of protection against XSS and CSRF are key aspects of the security of web applications in JavaScript, which help protect users and their data.","// The escaping of HTML \\nfunction escapeHtml(unsafe) {\\n  return unsafe        \\n    .replace(/&/g, \\"&amp;\\")        \\n    .replace(/</g, \\"&lt;\\")        \\n    .replace(/>/g, \\"&gt;\\")        \\n    .replace(/\\"/g, \\"&quot;\\")        \\n    .replace(/\'/g, \\"&#039;\\"); \\n}\\n\\n// An example of the checking of a CSRF token on the server (Node.js) \\napp.post(\'/update-settings\', (req, res) => {\\n  const token = req.body.csrfToken;    \\n  if (token !== req.session.csrfToken) {    \\n    return res.status(403).send(\'Forbidden\');    \\n  }  \\n  // The handling of the update of the settings \\n});"],[1008,"The safe handling of user input involves the validation, escaping and cleaning of the data before it is used in an application. It matters to check the input data on the client\'s and the server\'s side, in order to prevent possible attacks such as XSS and SQL injections.\\nWhen working with external APIs it is necessary to use secure methods of authentication, OAuth for example, and also to check and filter the responses from the API, in order to avoid handling malicious data.","The handling of user input and work with external APIs are critically important aspects of developing secure web applications. Here are the main principles that have to be taken into account:\\nThe handling of user input:\\n\\n- Validation: Check user input for the presence of the values and formats expected. If an email is expected, for example, use regular expressions to check the conformity.\\n\\n- Escaping and cleaning: All of the data that comes from users has to be escaped and cleaned before being displayed on the page. That will prevent the possibility of malicious code being carried out (XSS, for example).\\n\\n- Filtering: Use white lists for the permissible values, especially when working with important data such as addresses or users\' names.\\n\\nWork with external APIs:\\n\\n- Secure authentication: Use secure methods of authentication, such as OAuth 2.0, to access external APIs. That will help protect your credentials and your users\' data.\\n\\n- The checking of the data: Always check and filter the data received from external APIs. Even if you trust the API, do not forget the possibility of malicious data being sent.\\n\\n- The handling of errors: Handle the errors arising when working with an API correctly. Do not reveal too much information about the application\'s internal mechanisms, so as not to give attackers additional hints.\\n\\nBy following these principles you can substantially raise the security of your web application and protect it from the attacks connected with user input and external APIs.","function validateEmail(email) {\\n  const re = /^[^\\\\s@]+@[^\\\\s@]+\\\\.[^\\\\s@]+$/;    \\n  return re.test(String(email).toLowerCase()); \\n}\\n\\nasync function fetchData(url) {\\n  try {      \\n    const response = await fetch(url);        \\n    if (!response.ok) {      \\n      throw new Error(\'Network response was not ok\');        \\n    }        \\n      const data = await response.json();    \\n      // The handling of the data    \\n    } catch (error) {      \\n      console.error(\'Fetch error:\', error);  \\n      // The handling of the errors   \\n  } \\n}"],[1009,"Subresource Integrity (SRI) is a security mechanism that lets browsers check that the external resources loaded (scripts and styles, for example) have not been changed. To use SRI it is necessary to add the `integrity` attribute to `<script>` or `<link>` tags, stating the hash sum of the file being loaded. If the file has been changed, the browser will not load it, which prevents the execution of potentially malicious code.","Subresource Integrity (SRI) is an important mechanism that helps protect web applications from the attacks connected with the substitution of loaded resources, such as JavaScript files or CSS styles. When a web page loads resources from external sources, there is a risk that those resources may be changed by attackers. SRI helps guarantee that the file loaded corresponds to a particular integrity.\\nHow does it work? SRI uses hash sums to check the integrity of the resources loaded. A hash sum is a string obtained as the result of applying a cryptographic function to the content of a file. The browser loads the file and computes its hash sum, then compares it with the one stated in the `integrity` attribute.\\nAn example of the use of SRI: Let us look at an example in which jQuery is loaded from a CDN:\\n\\nIn this example the `integrity` attribute contains the hash sum for a specific version of jQuery. If the file changes, the browser will not load it and will give an error, protecting the user from potentially malicious code.\\nThe advantages of using SRI:\\n\\n- Protection against the substitution of resources.\\n\\n- The raising of trust in the resources loaded.\\n\\n- The improvement of the overall security of web applications.\\n\\nRecommendations:\\n\\n- Always use SRI for external resources.\\n\\n- Generate the hashes using reliable algorithms, such as SHA-256.\\n\\n- Update the hashes when the resources loaded change.\\n\\nThe use of Subresource Integrity (SRI) is a simple but effective way of raising the security of your web application and protecting users\' data.","<script src=\\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js\\"        \\n  integrity=\\"sha384-KyZXEAg3QhqLMpG8r+Knujsl5+5hb7J4aU+eW1B5E1jInC1L3ub2hwXcJ5BR1S5\\"        \\n  crossorigin=\\"anonymous\\">\\n<\/script>"],[1010,"Service Workers are scripts working in the background, which let web applications work in offline mode and cache resources to speed up the loading. To use them it is necessary to register the Service Worker in the application\'s main script and then use the caching API to save the resources. That lets users continue using the application even when there is no internet connection.","Service Workers are a powerful tool that lets developers build faster, more responsive web applications. They work in the background and are able to intercept network requests, which gives you the ability to cache resources and ensure applications work in offline mode.\\nHow does it work? A Service Worker is a special JavaScript file that can intercept requests to the network, cache resources and manage them. That lets an application load faster and work even if the network is unavailable.\\nThe registration of a Service Worker: The registration of a Service Worker is carried out in your application\'s main script. For example:\\n\\nThe caching of resources: In a Service Worker you can use the caching API to save resources. An example:\\n\\nIn this example, when the Service Worker is installed the caching of the main resources happens. On the subsequent requests the presence of the resource in the cache is checked first, and if it is found the cached response is returned.\\nThe advantages of using Service Workers:\\n\\n- The improvement of applications\' performance and responsiveness.\\n\\n- Ensuring that they work in offline mode.\\n\\n- The ability to work with background synchronisation and push notifications.\\n\\nRecommendations:\\n\\n- Keep an eye on the updates of the Service Worker in order to ensure the currency of the cached resources.\\n\\n- Use a caching strategy appropriate to your application — caching only for the resources that change rarely, for example.\\n\\nThe use of Service Workers opens up new possibilities for building modern web applications with an excellent user experience.","if (\'serviceWorker\' in navigator) {\\n  window.addEventListener(\'load\', () => {    \\n    navigator.serviceWorker.register(\'/service-worker.js\')            \\n      .then(registration => {         \\n        console.log(\'The Service Worker has been registered with the scope:\', registration.scope);            \\n      })            \\n      .catch(error => {           \\n        console.error(\'An error of the registration of the Service Worker:\', error);            \\n      });    \\n  }); \\n}\\n\\nself.addEventListener(\'install\', event => {\\n  event.waitUntil(        \\n    caches.open(\'my-cache\').then(cache => {      \\n        return cache.addAll([                \\n          \'/\',                \\n          \'/index.html\',                \\n          \'/styles.css\',                \\n          \'/script.js\',                \\n          \'/image.png\'           \\n        ]);        \\n    })    \\n  ); \\n}); \\nself.addEventListener(\'fetch\', event => {\\n    event.respondWith(    \\n        caches.match(event.request).then(response => {      \\n              return response || fetch(event.request);       \\n        })    \\n  ); \\n});"],[1011,"WebSockets is a protocol that provides two-way communication between a client and a server over one and the same TCP connection. It lets you send data in real time, which makes it ideal for applications requiring a constant exchange of data, such as chats or online games. To use WebSockets it is necessary to create a WebSocket client on the client\'s side and a server that will handle the connections and the messages.","WebSockets is a powerful protocol, letting you establish a persistent connection between a client and a server, ensuring the two-way exchange of data. That makes it especially useful for applications requiring updates in real time, such as chats, trading platforms and games.\\nHow does it work? A WebSocket connection begins with an HTTP request, after which the move to the WebSocket protocol happens. After that the connection stays open, letting messages be exchanged without the need for repeated authentication.\\nThe creation of a WebSocket client: On the client\'s side you can create a WebSocket client as follows:\\n\\nIn this example a connection with the WebSocket server is established and a message is sent. A handler for receiving messages from the server is also added.\\nThe creation of a WebSocket server: On the server\'s side you can use Node.js with the `ws` library to handle WebSocket connections:\\n\\nIn this example a WebSocket server is created, which handles the incoming connections and messages. The server responds to the client by sending the message received back.\\nThe advantages of using WebSockets:\\n\\n- The two-way exchange of data in real time.\\n\\n- The reduction of latency, since the connection stays open.\\n\\n- The effective use of resources, since there are fewer overheads compared with repeated HTTP requests.\\n\\nRecommendations:\\n\\n- Handle errors and the closing of connections in order to raise reliability.\\n\\n- Use security protocols such as WSS (WebSocket Secure) to protect the data.\\n\\nWebSockets are an important tool for building interactive, responsive web applications, ensuring the effective exchange of data between a client and a server in real time.","const socket = new WebSocket(\'ws://localhost:8080\'); \\nsocket.addEventListener(\'open\', (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'The connection has been established\');    \\n  socket.send(\'Hello, server!\'); \\n}); \\nsocket.addEventListener(\'message\', (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'A message from the server:\', event.data); \\n});\\n\\nconst WebSocket = require(\'ws\'); \\nconst wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 }); \\nwss.on(\'connection\', (ws) => {\\n  console.log(\'A new connection\');    \\n  ws.on(\'message\', (message) => {    \\n    console.log(\'A message has been received:\', message);        \\n    ws.send(\'A response from the server: \' + message);    \\n  }); \\n});"],[1012,"The Fetch API lets you load data from a server, while Streams let you handle it in parts, which is especially useful when working with large volumes of data. Instead of loading the whole file at once, you can use the `response.body.getReader()` method in order to read the data in parts, which lets you reduce the use of memory and speed up the handling. That lets you start handling the data without waiting for the loading to finish, which is especially useful for large files such as video or images.","The Fetch API is a modern interface for working with HTTP requests in JavaScript. It lets you easily load data from a server and handle it in an application. When working with large volumes of data, however — when loading large files, for example — the need for more effective handling can arise. Here the Streams API comes to the rescue.\\nHow does the Fetch API work? The Fetch API lets you make requests to a server and receive responses. An example of a request to obtain data:\\n\\nThe use of Streams: When you load large files, you can use the Stream API to read the data in parts. That lets you avoid loading the whole file into memory at once and handle it in parts instead. An example:\\n\\nThe advantages of using Streams:\\n\\n- The saving of memory: Handling the data in parts reduces the consumption of memory, which matters especially for large files.\\n\\n- Fast handling: You can start handling the data at once without waiting for it to finish loading, which improves an application\'s responsiveness.\\n\\n- An improved user experience: Users can see the results faster, especially when working with large files.\\n\\n- Application: The Streams API can be especially useful when loading images, video or large text files, where it matters to start handling the data as soon as possible.\\n\\nThe use of the Fetch API in combination with Streams lets you build more effective, responsive web applications, capable of handling large volumes of data.","fetch(\'https://example.com/large-file\')    \\n  .then(response => {   \\n       // The handling of the response        \\n       return response.blob(); // An example of obtaining a Blob object    \\n   })    \\n  .then(data => {        \\n    console.log(\'The data has been loaded:\', data);    \\n  })    \\n  .catch(error => {        \\n    console.error(\'Error:\', error);    \\n  });\\n\\nfetch(\'https://example.com/large-file\')    \\n  .then(response => {    \\n    const reader = response.body.getReader();        \\n      function read() {      \\n      return reader.read().then(({ done, value }) => {          \\n        if (done) {              \\n                console.log(\'The loading has finished\');                    \\n          return;                \\n        }\\n        // The handling of the portion of data received (value)                \\n        console.log(\'A portion of data has been received:\', value);                \\n        return read(); // The reading of the next portion            \\n            }); \\n    }\\n    return read(); // The beginning of the reading    \\n  })   \\n  .catch(error => {    \\n     console.error(\'Error:\', error);    \\n  });"],[1013,"WebRTC is a technology that lets browsers and mobile applications exchange audio, video and data in real time without the need to use third-party plugins. It uses three main components: `getUserMedia` for access to media devices, `RTCPeerConnection` to establish the connection and `RTCDataChannel` to transfer data.\\nWebRTC lets you build applications for video calls, chats and the exchange of files, ensuring low latency and high quality of communication.","WebRTC (Web Real-Time Communication) is a technology that lets you carry out the exchange of audio, video and data in real time between browsers and mobile applications without using third-party plugins. WebRTC is intended for building applications such as video calls, chats and the exchange of files, while ensuring low latency and high quality of communication.\\nThe main components of WebRTC:\\n\\n- getUserMedia: This API lets you gain access to media devices such as the camera and the microphone, in order to capture audio and video.\\n\\n- RTCPeerConnection: This component establishes a direct connection between two clients for the transfer of audio and video.\\n\\n- RTCDataChannel: This API lets you exchange data between clients.\\n\\nAn example of the implementation of a video call: To build a simple application for video calls, it is necessary to carry out the following steps:\\nObtaining access to the camera and the microphone:\\n\\nThe establishment of the connection by means of RTCPeerConnection:\\n\\nThe exchange of SDP and ICE candidates between the clients in order to establish the connection.\\nThe application of RTCDataChannel for the exchange of data: If you want to exchange data (text messages, for example), you can use `RTCDataChannel`:\\n\\nThe advantages of WebRTC:\\n\\n- The absence of latency: WebRTC ensures minimal latency, which matters for real-time applications.\\n\\n- Working without plugins: WebRTC is supported by most modern browsers and requires no additional plugins to be installed.\\n\\n- High quality of communication: WebRTC uses compression technologies, which lets it ensure high quality of sound and video.\\n\\nThe use of WebRTC opens up new possibilities for building interactive, high-quality communication applications, such as video calls and the exchange of data in real time.","navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ video: true, audio: true })    \\n  .then(stream => {    \\n      const videoElement = document.querySelector(\'video\');        \\n      videoElement.srcObject = stream;    \\n  })    \\n  .catch(error => {     \\n     console.error(\'An error of access to the media devices:\', error);   \\n  });\\n\\nconst peerConnection = new RTCPeerConnection(); \\nstream.getTracks().forEach(track => {\\n  peerConnection.addTrack(track, stream); \\n}); \\npeerConnection.onicecandidate = event => {\\n  if (event.candidate) {        \\n    // Send the ICE candidate to the other client        \\n    console.log(\'An ICE candidate:\', event.candidate);    \\n  } \\n};\\n\\nconst dataChannel = peerConnection.createDataChannel(\'chat\'); \\ndataChannel.onopen = () => {\\n  console.log(\'The channel is open\');    \\n  dataChannel.send(\'Hello, world!\'); \\n}; \\ndataChannel.onmessage = event => {\\n  console.log(\'A message from the other client:\', event.data); \\n};"],[1014,"The geolocation API lets web applications determine the user\'s location by means of GPS, Wi-Fi or other technologies. By means of the `navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition()` method you can obtain the current location, while `watchPosition()` lets you track changes of location.\\nBesides that, the API of sensors such as accelerometers and gyroscopes lets you interact with the physical movements of the device, which can be used to build interactive applications such as games or augmented-reality applications.","The geolocation and sensor APIs are powerful tools for building interactive web applications that use data about the user\'s location and the physical characteristics of the device. These technologies let developers build more personalised and interesting applications.\\nGeolocation: The geolocation API provides the means for determining the user\'s location. That can be useful for various applications, such as maps, search services and social networks.\\nThe main methods of the geolocation API:\\n\\n- `getCurrentPosition()`: obtains the current location.\\n\\n- `watchPosition()`: tracks changes of location.\\n\\nAn example of the use of `getCurrentPosition()`:\\n\\nThe tracking of changes of location: Using `watchPosition()`, you can track changes of location in real time:\\n\\nThe sensor API: The API of sensors such as the accelerometer and the gyroscope lets you obtain data about the device\'s movement. That can be useful for building games, augmented-reality applications and other interactive applications.\\nAn example of the use of sensors:\\n\\nApplication:\\n\\n- Navigation applications: Use the data about the location to display routes and information about the nearest objects.\\n\\n- Games: Build games that react to the physical movements of the device.\\n\\n- Augmented-reality applications: Use geolocation and sensors to create interactive AR experiences.\\n\\nThe geolocation and sensor APIs open up new possibilities for building unique, interactive web applications, improving the user experience and the interaction.","if (navigator.geolocation) { \\n  navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(position => {   \\n    const { latitude, longitude } = position.coords;        \\n    console.log(`Your location: ${latitude}, ${longitude}`);    \\n  }, error => {    \\n    console.error(\'An error of obtaining the location:\', error);    \\n  }); \\n} else {\\n  console.log(\'Geolocation is not supported by your browser.\'); \\n}\\n\\nnavigator.geolocation.watchPosition(position => {\\n  const { latitude, longitude } = position.coords;    \\n  console.log(`The current location: ${latitude}, ${longitude}`); \\n});\\n\\nif (window.DeviceOrientationEvent) {\\n  window.addEventListener(\'deviceorientation\', event => {     \\n    const { alpha, beta, gamma } = event;        \\n    console.log(`The angle of orientation: alpha: ${alpha}, beta: ${beta}, gamma: ${gamma}`);    \\n  }); \\n} else {\\n  console.log(\'Orientation sensors are not supported by your device.\'); \\n}"],[1015,"Your own task queue lets you manage the order in which asynchronous operations are carried out. To implement such a queue you can use an array to hold the tasks and sort them by priority. When adding a task to the queue we state its priority, and then we start carrying out the tasks in the order of their priority, using `Promise.resolve()` for the asynchronous execution. That lets you manage tasks effectively and ensure they are carried out in the order needed.","The implementation of your own task queue can be useful in various scenarios, when the order in which asynchronous operations are carried out, or their priority, has to be managed. In a game application, for example, you may want important events to be handled before less important ones.\\nThe creation of a task queue: To create a task queue you can use an array. Every task will be an object containing a function to carry out and a priority.\\n\\nThe use of the task queue: Now you can add tasks to the queue and start carrying them out:\\n\\nIn this example the task with priority 2 will be carried out first, even though it was added later.\\nApplication: Your own task queues can be used in various applications where the management of asynchronous operations is required, for example:\\n\\n- In games, to manage events (the collision of objects, for example).\\n\\n- In web applications, to handle requests to an API with various levels of importance.\\n\\n- In UI applications, to manage animations or changes of state.\\n\\nThe implementation of a task queue with priorities thus lets you manage the execution of asynchronous operations more flexibly, improving an application\'s performance and responsiveness.","class TaskQueue {\\n  constructor() {     \\n    this.tasks = [];    \\n  }    \\n  addTask(fn, priority = 0) {     \\n    this.tasks.push({ fn, priority });  \\n    // We sort the tasks by priority (a greater priority is higher in the queue)            \\n    this.tasks.sort((a, b) => b.priority - a.priority);    \\n  }    \\n  async run() {    \\n    while (this.tasks.length > 0) {        \\n      const { fn } = this.tasks.shift(); // We extract the first task            \\n      await fn(); // We carry out the task        \\n    }    \\n  } \\n}\\n\\nconst queue = new TaskQueue(); \\nqueue.addTask(() => new Promise(resolve => {\\n  setTimeout(() => {   \\n    console.log(\'Task 1 (priority 1)\');        \\n    resolve();    \\n  }, 1000); \\n}), 1); \\nqueue.addTask(() => new Promise(resolve => {\\n  setTimeout(() => {     \\n    console.log(\'Task 2 (priority 2)\');        \\n    resolve();    \\n  }, 500); \\n}), 2); \\nqueue.run(); // We start the carrying out of the tasks"],[1016,"Blocking operations in JavaScript are those that stop the execution of the code and do not let the Event Loop handle other tasks. That can lead to the user interface hanging and to a reduction in an application\'s responsiveness.\\nTo avoid blocking operations, use asynchronous functions such as `setTimeout` and `Promise`, and APIs that let tasks be carried out in the background without blocking the main thread of execution.","In JavaScript every operation is carried out in a single thread, and that can lead to problems with performance if the working of the Event Loop is not taken into account. The Event Loop is the mechanism that manages the execution of asynchronous tasks and lets JavaScript handle events and callbacks.\\nBlocking operations: Blocking operations are operations that require a significant time to carry out and do not let the Event Loop continue handling other tasks. The carrying out of complex computations or operations of reading files, for example, can block the thread of execution, which will lead to the user interface hanging.\\nAn example of a blocking operation:\\n\\nIn this example the carrying out of the loop takes a long time, and `console.log(\'End\')` will not be carried out until the loop has finished. That leads to the interface hanging.\\nAvoiding blocking operations: To avoid blocking operations, asynchronous functions such as `setTimeout` and `Promise`, and APIs working in the background, should be used.\\nAn example of the use of `setTimeout`:\\n\\nHere `console.log(\'End\')` will be carried out immediately after `console.log(\'Start\')`, while the asynchronous operation will be carried out later, letting the Event Loop continue handling other tasks.\\nPractical recommendations:\\n\\n- Use asynchronous functions: Always try to carry out long operations asynchronously, in order to avoid blocking the main thread.\\n\\n- Break heavy operations up: If you have complex computations, consider the possibility of breaking them up into smaller tasks and using `setTimeout` or `requestAnimationFrame` to carry them out in stages.\\n\\n- Use Web Workers: For heavy computations consider the possibility of using Web Workers, which let you carry out code in a background thread without blocking the main thread.\\n\\nAn understanding of the influence of blocking operations on the Event Loop and the application of asynchronous approaches thus lets you build more responsive, performant web applications.","console.log(\'Start\'); \\n// A blocking operation \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {} \\nconsole.log(\'End\');\\n\\nconsole.log(\'Start\'); \\n// An asynchronous operation \\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'Async operation completed\'); \\n}, 0); \\nconsole.log(\'End\');"],[1017,"In V8 the caching of code divides into three types: the cold cache, the warm cache and the hot cache. The cold cache relates to code that has not been carried out before and has no optimisations. The warm cache contains code that has been carried out several times, and the engine can start to optimise it. The hot cache includes frequently carried out code, which has already been optimised and works faster, which substantially improves an application\'s performance.","In JavaScript the V8 engine uses various levels of caching to raise performance. An understanding of the cold, warm and hot cache can help developers optimise their applications better.\\n\\n- The cold cache:\\nThis is the initial stage, when the code has not yet been carried out. Code in the cold cache is carried out using basic interpretation without optimisations. Since this code has not been used before, it may be less efficient, and the performance may suffer.\\n\\n- The warm cache:\\nWhen a function or code is carried out several times, V8 starts to optimise it. That relates to the warm cache, which contains code that has been carried out but has not yet reached a high frequency of calls. At this stage the engine can apply some optimisations in order to improve the performance, but the code is still not fully optimised.\\n\\n- The hot cache:\\nCode that is carried out very often falls into the hot cache. V8 applies more aggressive optimisations to this code, using various techniques such as JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation. Code in the hot cache can be carried out substantially faster, since the engine has already gathered statistics about its execution and optimised it for the given environment.\\n\\nAn understanding of caching in V8 can help developers:\\n\\n- Optimise the code by singling out frequently used functions and minimising the carrying out of heavy operations.\\n\\n- Reduce the number of repeated computations and call functions with a high frequency, so that they fall into the hot cache.\\n\\n- Avoid the excessive dynamic generation of code, which can hinder effective caching.\\n\\nA knowledge of the cold, warm and hot cache in V8 thus lets you develop more effective, performant applications that make use of the engine\'s capabilities.","function example() {\\n  // The cold cache, the first call    \\n  console.log(\'Hello, World!\'); \\n} \\nexample();\\n\\nfunction example() {\\n  console.log(\'Hello, World!\'); \\n} \\n// The warm cache, the second call \\nexample(); \\nexample();\\n\\nfunction example() {\\n  console.log(\'Hello, World!\'); \\n} \\n// The hot cache, frequent calls \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n  example(); \\n}"],[1018,"JavaScript works on a single thread, which means it carries out code sequentially, one piece after another. It uses the Event Loop to handle asynchronous operations and events, however. When an asynchronous operation is carried out, it is put into the queue, and JavaScript continues carrying out the next code until the time comes to handle the asynchronous operation. Thus, although JavaScript does not use multithreading, it manages asynchronous tasks effectively.","JavaScript was originally created to be carried out in browsers, and its architecture is designed to be simple and effective.\\n\\n- The single-threaded model:\\nJavaScript is carried out in a single thread, which means that every operation is carried out sequentially. That makes development simpler, since developers do not have to worry about problems with the synchronisation of threads, such as data races.\\n\\nIn this example `Task 1` will be carried out before `Task 2`, and they cannot be carried out at the same time.\\n\\n- The Event Loop:\\nTo handle asynchronous operations JavaScript uses the Event Loop. When an asynchronous operation is carried out (a request to a server, for example), it is sent into the queue, and JavaScript continues carrying out the remaining operations. Once the asynchronous operation has finished, it is placed back into the event queue, and when the execution stack becomes empty the Event Loop handles that queue.\\n\\nIn this example `Async Task` will be carried out only after `Start` and `End` have finished executing.\\n\\n- Asynchronous operations:\\nJavaScript provides several methods for working with asynchronous code, such as:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`, `setInterval` for delays.\\n\\n- `Promises` for working with asynchronous actions.\\n\\n- `async/await`, to make working with promises simpler.\\n\\nHere the `fetchData` function will carry out a request to the API without blocking the main thread of execution.\\n\\nAn understanding of the working of threads in JavaScript will help developers:\\n\\n- Use asynchronous code effectively in order to improve performance.\\n\\n- Avoid the blocking of the interface by guaranteeing that long operations are carried out asynchronously.\\n\\n- Work with events and callbacks in order to organise an application\'s logic.\\n\\nUltimately, a knowledge of threads and of the Event Loop in JavaScript lets you build responsive, high-performance applications by managing asynchronous tasks effectively.","console.log(\'Task 1\'); \\nconsole.log(\'Task 2\');\\n\\nconsole.log(\'Start\'); \\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'Async Task\'); \\n}, 1000); \\nconsole.log(\'End\');\\n\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  console.log(\'Fetching data...\');    \\n  const data = await fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\');    \\n  const json = await data.json();    \\n  console.log(json); \\n} \\nfetchData();"],[1019,"The code inside a `<script>` tag is carried out synchronously and is not a macrotask. When the browser loads a page, it first carries out all of the synchronous code contained in the `<script>` tag, and only once that execution has finished does it move on to handling the other tasks in the queue, such as macrotasks and events. Macrotasks include asynchronous operations such as `setTimeout` or event handlers.","In JavaScript there are two categories of task that control how code is carried out: macrotasks and microtasks.\\n\\n- Macrotasks:\\nMacrotasks are larger blocks of work, which are put into a queue and carried out as the execution stack becomes free. Examples of macrotasks include:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- Event handlers (mouse clicks, for example)\\n\\n- Microtasks:\\nMicrotasks are smaller tasks, which have a higher priority and are handled after the current execution stack has been carried out but before the next macrotask is carried out.\\nExamples of microtasks include:\\n\\n- Promise handlers (`Promise.then` and `Promise.catch`)\\n\\n- `MutationObserver` handlers\\n\\n- Synchronous code:\\nCode inside a `<script>` tag is carried out synchronously. That means it will be carried out as soon as the browser meets the `<script>` tag, and until that code has finished the browser will not handle any other macrotasks or events.\\n\\nIn this example the message will be printed to the console at once, before the browser begins carrying out any macrotasks.\\n\\nThe order of execution:\\nLet us look at the following code:\\n\\nHere `Script Start` will be printed first, then `Script End`, and then, once every synchronous operation has finished, the `Timeout Task` macrotask will be carried out.\\n\\nA knowledge that the code inside a `<script>` tag is not a macrotask helps developers:\\n\\n- Organise the order of the execution of code and of asynchronous operations correctly.\\n\\n- Avoid unexpected delays and blockages in applications.\\n\\n- Improve performance by managing the execution of tasks.\\n\\nAn understanding of the differences between macrotasks, microtasks and synchronous code thus lets developers build more effective, responsive web applications.","<script>\\n  console.log(\'This is a script tag\'); \\n<\/script>\\n\\n<script>\\n  console.log(\'Script Start\'); \\n  setTimeout(() => {    \\n      console.log(\'Timeout Task\');    \\n  }, 0);    \\n  console.log(\'Script End\'); \\n<\/script>"],[1020,"Rendering in the browser is responsible for displaying a web page, including the markup, the styles and the behaviour. The event loop manages the execution of JavaScript code and the handling of events by putting asynchronous tasks into a queue.\\nWhen the browser finishes the current execution stack, it checks the event queue and carries out the tasks, which can suspend the rendering if there are heavy operations.\\nAn understanding of this connection helps developers avoid the blocking of the interface and improve applications\' responsiveness.","The rendering of a web page is a process that includes several stages: the parsing of the HTML, the building of the DOM tree, the application of the CSS and the creation of the Render Tree, and then the painting of the page. These stages are carried out in the browser, and their efficiency is directly connected with how the event loop works.\\nWhat is the Event Loop?\\nThe event loop is the mechanism that lets JavaScript carry out asynchronous operations, such as the handling of events and the carrying out of timeouts, without blocking the main thread of execution. It supports the single-threaded model by handling the code in turn.\\nThe connection between rendering and the Event Loop:\\nWhen the browser renders a page, it carries out the JavaScript and updates the display on the basis of the changes. If the JavaScript carries out heavy operations, that can delay the rendering, since the browser cannot update the screen while the scripts are being carried out. For example:\\n\\nIn this example the carrying out of the loop blocks the rendering, and users will not be able to see the updates of the interface until the loop has finished.\\nAvoiding blockages:\\nTo avoid the blocking of the interface, developers can break heavy operations up into smaller tasks, using `setTimeout` or `requestAnimationFrame`. That lets the browser carry out the rendering between the carrying out of the operations:\\n\\nAn understanding of the connection between rendering and the event loop lets developers:\\n\\n- Optimise the code so that it does not block the rendering.\\n\\n- Use asynchronous operations to raise the interface\'s responsiveness.\\n\\n- Build smoother user interfaces by avoiding delays while scripts are carried out.\\n\\nThe correct understanding of how the event loop affects the rendering thus helps improve performance and the user experience.","console.log(\'Start\');\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {\\n  // An intensive operation \\n} \\nconsole.log(\'End\');\\n\\nfunction intensiveTask() {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1e8; i++) {\\n    // Light operations    \\n  }    \\n  if (moreTasks) {    \\n    setTimeout(intensiveTask, 0); // Lets the rendering finish    \\n  } \\n} \\nintensiveTask();"],[1021,"The event loop can suspend the parsing of HTML if JavaScript is carried out while the page is loading. When the browser meets a `<script>` tag, it stops the parsing and carries out the script, which can slow the loading of the page down. To avoid that, developers can use the `async` or `defer` attributes, which let scripts be loaded and carried out asynchronously without blocking the parsing.","The parsing of HTML is the process in which the browser reads the HTML code, builds the DOM tree and prepares the page for rendering. This process can be hindered if `<script>` tags, which require JavaScript to be carried out, are met in the HTML.\\nHow the Event Loop works:\\nThe event loop lets JavaScript carry out asynchronous operations by adding them to the event queue. When the browser begins to parse the HTML and meets a `<script>` tag, it stops the parsing and begins to carry out the code inside the tag. That can lead to delays, especially if the script takes a long time to carry out.\\n\\nIn this example the browser will not be able to continue the parsing until the execution of the script has finished, which will slow down the display of the page\'s content.\\nThe use of `async` and `defer`:\\nTo avoid blockages during the parsing, developers can use the `async` and `defer` attributes in the `<script>` tag.\\n\\n- `async`: The script is loaded asynchronously and is carried out immediately after loading, which can break the order of execution if the scripts depend on one another.\\n\\n- `defer`: The script is loaded asynchronously but is carried out only after the parsing of the HTML has finished. That guarantees that the order of execution will be preserved.\\n\\nThe use of `async` and `defer` lets developers:\\n\\n- Improve the performance of the loading of pages.\\n\\n- Avoid blockages and delays during the parsing of the HTML.\\n\\n- Guarantee the correct order of the execution of the scripts where necessary.\\n\\nA knowledge of the influence of the event loop on the parsing of HTML and of the ways of optimising it thus helps developers build faster, more responsive web applications.","<html>\\n  <head>    \\n    <script>    \\n      // This code blocks the parsing            \\n      for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {} \\n    <\/script>   \\n  </head>    \\n  <body>    \\n        <p>Hello World</p>    \\n  </body> \\n</html>\\n\\n<script src=\\"script1.js\\" async><\/script> \\n<script src=\\"script2.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"script1.js\\" defer><\/script> \\n<script src=\\"script2.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[1022,"Short polling is the method by which the client periodically sends requests to the server at particular intervals in order to check for the presence of new data. Long polling, on the other hand, lets the client send a request and the server holds that request open until new data appears, after which it sends a response. Long polling is more effective than short polling, since it reduces the number of unnecessary requests and the latency.","Polling is a technique used by a client to obtain data from a server. There are two main approaches: short polling and long polling.\\n\\n- Short polling:\\nShort polling is a simple technique in which the client sends a request to the server at particular intervals of time, in order to check for the presence of new data.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example the client will send a request to the server every 5 seconds. If no new data has appeared, the client will simply receive the old data or an empty response. That can lead to superfluous requests and increase the load on the server.\\n\\n- Long polling:\\nLong polling is a more effective method, in which the client sends a request to the server and holds the connection open until the server has prepared new data.\\nAn example:\\n\\nHere the client sends a request, and the server waits until new data appears before responding. Once the response has been received, the client immediately initiates a new request. That reduces the number of unnecessary requests and the latency, since the server responds only when there is new data.\\n\\nA comparison:\\n\\n- Short polling: Simpler but less effective, since it can generate many requests, especially if the data is updated rarely.\\n\\n- Long polling: More complex but a more effective method for applications where the data can change often, since it lets the client receive the data immediately when it becomes available.\\n\\nApplication:\\n\\n- Short polling can be appropriate for applications with low requirements as regards the updating of data.\\n\\n- Long polling suits real-time applications, such as chats, notifications or updates of statuses, where the data changes often and minimal latency is required.\\n\\nThe choice between long and short polling thus depends on your application\'s requirements and on the frequency with which the data on the server is updated.","setInterval(() => {\\n  fetch(\'/data\')        \\n    .then(response => response.json())        \\n    .then(data => {        \\n        // The handling of the new data            \\n        console.log(data);        \\n  }); \\n}, 5000); // A request every 5 seconds\\n\\nfunction longPoll() {\\n  fetch(\'/data\')        \\n    .then(response => response.json())        \\n    .then(data => {     \\n         // The handling of the new data            \\n         console.log(data);            \\n         longPoll(); // Request the new data again        \\n     })        \\n     .catch(err => {       \\n         console.error(err);            \\n         setTimeout(longPoll, 5000); // Repeat the request after 5 seconds in the event of an error        \\n   }); \\n} \\nlongPoll();"],[1023,"JavaScript uses a mechanism called the \\"lexical environment\\" to look for references to variables in a particular scope. When a function is called, a new lexical environment is created, which includes the references to the variables declared in the parent scopes. If a variable is not found in the current scope, the engine continues the search in the parent scopes until it finds the variable or reaches the global scope.","The JavaScript engine uses the lexical environment to manage variables and their scope. A lexical environment is a data structure that holds the variables, functions and other values connected with a particular execution context. An understanding of how the engine looks for references to variables helps you avoid errors and organise code better.\\nThe definition of a lexical environment:\\nA lexical environment consists of two main components:\\n\\n- The Environment Record: Contains every variable and function declared in the given scope.\\n\\n- The Outer Environment Reference: Points to the parent lexical environment.\\n\\nWhen a function is created, a new lexical environment is created, which includes every variable from the parent environment.\\nThe search for variables:\\n\\n- When the JavaScript engine looks for a variable, it carries out the following steps:\\n\\n- First it checks the current lexical environment (the local scope).\\n\\n- If the variable is not found, the engine moves to the parent environment and looks there.\\n\\n- This process continues until the variable is found or the global scope is reached.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nIn this example, when the `inner` function tries to gain access to the variables, the engine carries out the following search:\\n\\n- First it looks for `c` in its own local environment (it finds it).\\n\\n- Then it looks for `b` in the parent `outer` environment (it finds it).\\n\\n- Finally, it looks for `a` in the global environment (it finds it).\\n\\nThe problem of \\"hoisting\\":\\nIt matters to remember that the declarations of variables by means of `var` are hoisted to the top of the function or of the global context. That means a variable can be available in a function even if it was declared later in the code:\\n\\nIn this case, although `x` is declared after the first `console.log`, the JavaScript engine hoists the declaration, and `x` exists, although it has no value until the assignment.\\nApplication:\\n\\n- An understanding of how the engine looks for variables in a scope helps you:\\n\\n- Avoid the errors connected with scope.\\n\\n- Organise code correctly, using closures and functions.\\n\\n- Optimise performance by minimising access to global variables.\\n\\nA knowledge of how JavaScript manages scope and the search for variables thus lets developers write cleaner, more predictable code.","let a = 1; \\nfunction outer() {\\n  let b = 2;    \\n  function inner() {    \\n    let c = 3;        \\n    console.log(a, b, c); // Here there is access to a, b and c    \\n  }    \\n  inner(); \\n} \\nouter();\\n\\nconsole.log(x); // undefined \\nvar x = 5; \\nconsole.log(x); // 5"],[1024,"`AbortController` is a built-in object in JavaScript that lets you cancel asynchronous operations, such as requests through `fetch`. It works together with `AbortSignal`, which is passed into the asynchronous operation. If you call the controller\'s `abort()` method, the operation is interrupted and its result will no longer be handled.","`AbortController` and `AbortSignal` were introduced for the convenient management of asynchronous processes in JavaScript.\\n`AbortController` creates a signal that can be passed into functions such as `fetch`. This signal lets you stop the execution of a request when it is no longer needed — if the user has moved to another page, for example, or the operation is taking too long.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nIn this example the request is cancelled after 3 seconds, and that prevents the handling of a result that may arrive later. That is useful for preventing memory leaks and superfluous computations when tasks are cancelled.","const controller = new AbortController(); \\nconst signal = controller.signal; \\nfetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', { signal })  \\n  .then(response => response.json())  \\n  .then(data => console.log(data))  \\n  .catch(err => {\\n    if (err.name === \'AbortError\') {   \\n      console.log(\'Fetch aborted!\');    \\n    } else {   \\n      console.error(\'Fetch error:\', err);    \\n    }  \\n  }); \\n// Let us cancel the request after 3 seconds \\nsetTimeout(() => {  controller.abort(); }, 3000);"],[1025,"The optimisation of images includes several methods: the compression of images (the use of the WebP, JPEG or PNG formats), the loading of images only as they are needed (lazy loading), the choice of adaptive images with different sizes for different devices, the use of a CDN for fast delivery and the addition of the `alt` attribute to raise accessibility.","The optimisation of images is an important aspect of raising a site\'s performance. Several key methods include:\\nThe compression of images: The use of tools for compressing images (TinyPNG, for example) reduces their size without a noticeable loss of quality. For web pages it is recommended to use modern image formats such as WebP, which provide better compression than the traditional formats such as JPEG or PNG.\\nAdaptive images: You can provide images of different sizes for different screens, using the `srcset` attribute:\\n\\nThat lets images corresponding to the device\'s resolution be loaded, saving traffic.\\nLazy loading: Images are loaded only when they fall into the user\'s visible area. That can be done by means of the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute:\\n\\nThe use of a CDN: Holding images on a Content Delivery Network (CDN) lets them be delivered faster, since the requests will be handled by the nearest servers.\\nOptimisation through CSS and SVG: Sometimes raster images can be replaced with vector ones (SVG), especially for icons and simple graphics.\\nThese methods help substantially shorten the loading time of a page and improve SEO.","<img src=\\"small.jpg\\" srcset=\\"large.jpg 1024w, medium.jpg 640w, small.jpg 320w\\" alt=\\"Example Image\\">\\n\\n<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Example Image\\">"],[1026,"A simple slider can be created by means of JavaScript by manipulating DOM elements. The main idea consists in changing the visibility or the offset of the pictures in turn. You can use the `setInterval()` method or events for the automatic changing of the slides, and also add buttons for manual navigation between the slides.","A slider is a popular element of an interface, which lets users view several elements (images, for example) in turn. To implement it you can use HTML for the structure, CSS for the styles and JavaScript for the logic of switching the slides.\\nAn example of a simple slider:\\n\\nThis example shows a simple slider, which switches the images when the \\"Next\\" and \\"Prev\\" buttons are pressed. We change the classes of the slides for their visibility. By means of this structure you can add additional functions, such as the automatic changing of the slides or smooth animations.","<div class=\\"slider\\">\\n  <div class=\\"slides\\">\\n    <img src=\\"image1.jpg\\" class=\\"slide\\" alt=\\"Slide 1\\">\\n    <img src=\\"image2.jpg\\" class=\\"slide\\" alt=\\"Slide 2\\">\\n    <img src=\\"image3.jpg\\" class=\\"slide\\" alt=\\"Slide 3\\">\\n  </div>\\n  <button id=\\"prev\\">Prev</button>\\n  <button id=\\"next\\">Next</button>\\n</div>\\n<script>\\n  let currentSlide = 0;\\n  const slides = document.querySelectorAll(\'.slide\');\\n  document.getElementById(\'next\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    slides[currentSlide].classList.remove(\'active\');\\n    currentSlide = (currentSlide + 1) % slides.length;\\n    slides[currentSlide].classList.add(\'active\');\\n  });\\n  document.getElementById(\'prev\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    slides[currentSlide].classList.remove(\'active\');\\n    currentSlide = (currentSlide - 1 + slides.length) % slides.length;\\n    slides[currentSlide].classList.add(\'active\');\\n  });\\n  slides[currentSlide].classList.add(\'active\');\\n<\/script>\\n<style>\\n  .slide { display: none; }\\n  .slide.active { display: block; }\\n</style>"],[1027,"To create a simple modal window in pure JavaScript you have to hide the window by default and show it on a particular action — the pressing of a button, for example. For that you can use CSS styles for the display and hiding, and JavaScript to add/remove those styles during the user\'s interaction — on a click on a button or on the closing icon, for example.","A modal window is a pop-up window that is displayed over the page\'s main content and is often used to display important information or to obtain input from the user. In pure JavaScript it is implemented through manipulations of the DOM and the changing of styles.\\nAn example of a simple modal window:\\n\\nHere the modal window is hidden by default (`display: none`). When the button is pressed the modal window opens, and when the cross or the area outside the window is clicked it closes. It is a simple way of implementing a modal window without using libraries.","<!-- HTML -->\\n<button id=\\"openModal\\">Open the modal window</button>\\n<div id=\\"modal\\" class=\\"modal\\">\\n  <div class=\\"modal-content\\">\\n    <span id=\\"closeModal\\" class=\\"close\\">&times;</span>\\n    <p>This is a modal window</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n<!-- CSS -->\\n<style>\\n  .modal { display: none; position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); }\\n  .modal-content { position: relative; margin: 15% auto; padding: 20px; width: 80%; background-color: white; }\\n  .close { position: absolute; top: 10px; right: 20px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 20px; }\\n</style>\\n<!-- JavaScript -->\\n<script>\\n  const modal = document.getElementById(\'modal\');\\n  const openModal = document.getElementById(\'openModal\');\\n  const closeModal = document.getElementById(\'closeModal\');\\n  openModal.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    modal.style.display = \'block\';\\n  });\\n  closeModal.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    modal.style.display = \'none\';\\n  });\\n  window.addEventListener(\'click\', (e) => {\\n    if (e.target == modal) {\\n      modal.style.display = \'none\';\\n    }\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[1028,"The `use strict` directive activates strict mode in JavaScript, which helps you write safer, more predictable code. It prevents the use of unsafe or obsolete capabilities of the language, such as the automatic creation of global variables.\\nStrict mode helps you find errors faster and improves performance in certain cases.","`use strict` is a directive introduced in ECMAScript 5, which switches strict mode on. This mode makes JavaScript work more predictably, making the debugging of code simpler and preventing many errors.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn strict mode JavaScript will throw an error, because the variable `x` was not declared by means of `var`, `let` or `const`. Without strict mode this error could pass unnoticed, since JavaScript would automatically create a global variable.\\nStrict mode also forbids:\\n\\n- The duplication of the names of parameters in a function.\\n\\n- The use of certain words reserved for future versions of JavaScript.\\n\\n- The changing of the values of immutable objects such as `NaN`.\\n\\nThe application of strict mode makes code more predictable and helps you avoid hidden errors.","\'use strict\';\\n\\nfunction myFunction() {\\n  x = 10; // An error: the variable x has not been declared\\n  console.log(x);\\n}\\nmyFunction();"],[1029,"The temporal dead zone (TDZ) is the period of time from the beginning of the scope of a variable declared by means of `let` or `const` to the moment of its initialisation. In the TDZ the variables are inaccessible, and an attempt to access them will raise an error. That helps you avoid unpredictable behaviour when working with variables.","The temporal dead zone is a concept connected with the variables declared by means of `let` and `const`. Unlike the variables declared with `var`, which are automatically initialised with the value `undefined`, variables with `let` and `const` are not initialised until the execution of the code reaches the line where they are declared. Until that moment they are in the TDZ, and an attempt to access them will lead to a `ReferenceError`.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example `var` behaves as though it had been «hoisted» to the beginning, but the variable `myLet` is inaccessible until its actual initialisation. That is precisely the temporal dead zone — the section of code where the variable has been declared but is not yet available to be used.\\nThe TDZ helps you avoid errors where variables are used before their initialisation, which makes code more reliable and predictable.","console.log(myVar); // undefined \\nvar myVar = 5; \\nconsole.log(myLet); // ReferenceError: Cannot access \'myLet\' before initialization \\nlet myLet = 10;"],[1030,"Boxing is the process by which primitive values (numbers or strings, for example) are temporarily converted into objects in order to have access to methods as objects do. Unboxing is the reverse process, where the object is returned back into a primitive value. These processes happen automatically in JavaScript when you try to use methods on primitive data types such as strings or numbers.","JavaScript has two types of data: primitive (`string`, `number`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint`, for example) and objects. Primitive values usually have no methods, but in certain situations they can be used as though they were objects.\\nThe process of boxing consists in JavaScript automatically creating temporary object versions of primitives when you call methods on primitives. For example:\\n\\nIn this example the string `str` is a primitive value, but when the `toUpperCase()` method is called JavaScript temporarily converts it into a `String` object behind the scenes, in order to call the method. Once the method has been carried out, unboxing happens and the object becomes a primitive again.\\nThis process makes work with primitives simpler by providing the ability to use methods without explicitly creating objects. An example of the process of boxing:\\n\\nAlthough `num` is a number, JavaScript temporarily \\"boxes\\" it into an object of the `Number` type, so that you can call the `toString()` method.\\nThe use of boxing lets you improve the convenience of working with primitive data, but it matters to remember that these are temporary objects, and abusing it can have a negative effect on performance.","let str = \\"hello\\"; \\nconsole.log(str.toUpperCase()); // \\"HELLO\\"\\n\\nlet num = 42; \\nconsole.log(num.toString()); // \\"42\\""],[1031,"The use of constructors such as `new String` or `new Number` is not recommended, since they create an object instead of a primitive. Objects behave differently from primitive data types and can lead to unexpected results in comparisons and other operations. Primitive values (strings or numbers, for example) should be used directly without constructors.","In JavaScript there are primitive data types such as strings, numbers and boolean values. When you use such values, they behave simply and predictably. If you use constructors such as `new String`, however, you create an object rather than a primitive. That can lead to unexpected behaviour, especially during comparisons.\\nAn example of the problem:\\n\\nIn this example, although the values of the strings seem identical, the `===` operator returns `false`, because `str2` is an object while `str1` is a primitive. That can cause confusion and bugs in code, since objects are compared by reference while primitives are compared by value.\\nObjects created by means of constructors also take up more memory and reduce performance. It is therefore recommended to avoid using them and to work with primitives directly:","let str1 = \\"hello\\"; \\nlet str2 = new String(\\"hello\\"); \\nconsole.log(str1 === str2); // false, because str2 is an object while str1 is a primitive\\n\\nlet str = \\"hello\\"; // instead of new String(\\"hello\\")"],[1032,"Records and tuples are new immutable data structures in JavaScript, which hold data in the format of an object and an array respectively. Unlike ordinary objects and arrays, records and tuples are immutable (they cannot be changed after creation) and are compared by value rather than by reference. That makes them more reliable and predictable when working with data.","Records and tuples are new data structures proposed for JavaScript, which differ from traditional objects and arrays in that they are immutable and are compared by value rather than by reference.\\nRecords:\\nRecords are similar to objects, but they are immutable. That means that once a record has been created its fields cannot be changed. That is convenient for creating safe, predictable data that cannot be accidentally changed.\\n\\nTuples:\\nTuples are similar to arrays, but they too are immutable. They can contain data of different types but, unlike arrays, cannot be changed after creation.\\n\\nThe differences from ordinary objects and arrays:\\n\\n- Immutability: Ordinary objects and arrays can be changed, whereas records and tuples cannot. That makes them more reliable for holding permanent data.\\n\\n- Comparison by value: Ordinary objects and arrays are compared by reference, which can lead to errors when working with identical data located in different places in memory. Records and tuples, by contrast, are compared by value, which makes the comparison simpler and more predictable.\\n\\nThese structures can be useful in situations where the immutability of the data matters — when working with state in applications or when passing data between functions, for example.","let record1 = #{ name: \\"Alice\\", age: 30 }; \\nlet record2 = #{ name: \\"Alice\\", age: 30 }; \\nconsole.log(record1 === record2); // true, because records are compared by value\\n\\nlet tuple1 = #[1, 2, 3]; \\nlet tuple2 = #[1, 2, 3]; \\nconsole.log(tuple1 === tuple2); // true, tuples are also compared by value"],[1033,"A `Promise` is an object that works with a single value or error, and its result can be obtained only once. It is ideal for one-off asynchronous operations such as requests to an API. An `Observable` lets you work with multiple values and manage subscriptions to a stream of data, which makes it useful for events, streams of data or long operations. The main difference is in the multiplicity of the values and in the capabilities for managing the stream.","`Promise` and `Observable` are two different approaches to working with asynchronous code in JavaScript.\\n\\n- `Promise`:\\nA `Promise` represents a finite operation, which will either finish successfully (`resolve` will be carried out) or finish with an error (`reject` will be carried out). When you create a `Promise`, you expect a single event — the completion of an API request, for example:\\n\\nA `Promise` is convenient to use when you expect only one result.\\n\\n- `Observable`:\\nAn `Observable` is used to work with streams of data that can change over time. That lets you subscribe to events and receive multiple values. The main advantage is the management of subscriptions, cancellation and multiple updates:\\n\\nIn this example the data is passed several times, which makes an `Observable` useful for working with real streams, such as the user\'s events, WebSocket connections or continuous updates of data.\\n\\nWhere to apply them:\\n\\n- Use a `Promise` when you have to obtain a single value or the result of an asynchronous operation (the loading of data from a server, for example).\\n\\n- Use an `Observable` when you have to work with multiple values over time or when the ability to cancel a subscription to a stream of data matters.","const fetchData = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => {  \\n    resolve(\'Data loaded\');  \\n  }, 1000); \\n}); \\nfetchData.then(data => console.log(data)); // \\"Data loaded\\"\\n\\nconst { Observable } = require(\'rxjs\'); \\nconst dataStream = new Observable(observer => {\\n  observer.next(\'First value\');  \\n  setTimeout(() => observer.next(\'Second value\'), 1000);  \\n  setTimeout(() => observer.complete(), 2000); \\n}); \\ndataStream.subscribe({\\n  next: value => console.log(value),  \\n  complete: () => console.log(\'Stream completed\'), \\n});"],[1034,"The sequential execution of asynchronous functions means that every following function begins only after the previous one has finished, whereas parallel execution lets several functions be carried out at the same time.\\nThe `Promise.all()` method starts several promises in parallel and returns the result when all of them have finished successfully, while `Promise.allSettled()` returns the result regardless of whether every promise finished with success or with an error.","Asynchronous functions can be carried out either sequentially or in parallel:\\n\\n- Sequential execution:\\nWith sequential execution every promise or asynchronous operation begins only once the previous one has finished:\\n\\nIn this example `task2` will start only after `task1` has finished, and so on. That is useful when the tasks depend on one another.\\n\\n- Parallel execution:\\nParallel execution lets you start several tasks at the same time:\\n\\nHere all three tasks will begin at the same time, which can substantially shorten the overall execution time if the tasks are independent.\\n\\n- `Promise.all()` takes an array of promises and returns a new `Promise`, which will be fulfilled when every promise has finished successfully. If one of the promises finishes with an error, the whole `Promise.all()` will finish with an error.\\n\\n- `Promise.allSettled()` waits for every promise to finish, regardless of whether they finished successfully or with an error. That is useful when every result has to be handled, even if some tasks finished with an error:\\n\\nEvery result in the array will be an object with a `status` field (success or error) and a value or an error.\\n\\nThe use of parallel execution can substantially speed a program\'s working up, especially when the tasks do not depend on one another.","async function runSequentially() {\\n  await task1();  \\n  await task2();  \\n  await task3(); \\n}\\n\\nasync function runInParallel() {\\n  await Promise.all([task1(), task2(), task3()]); \\n}\\n\\nPromise.all([fetchData1(), fetchData2()])  \\n  .then(results => console.log(results))  \\n  .catch(error => console.log(error));\\n\\nPromise.allSettled([fetchData1(), fetchData2()])  \\n  .then(results => console.log(results));"],[1035,"Property descriptors are special objects that describe the attributes of an object\'s property, such as the ability to change, enumerate or delete it. Using descriptors, you can control precisely how an object\'s properties will behave. You can make a property immutable or hide it from iteration through a loop, for example.","In JavaScript the properties of objects have not only values but also particular attributes, called descriptors. These descriptors give you the ability to control how a property behaves in an object.\\nThere are two types of descriptor:\\n\\n- A data descriptor: Describes an ordinary property with its value.\\n\\n- `value`: the current value of the property.\\n\\n- `writable`: whether the value of the property can be changed.\\n\\n- `enumerable`: whether the property will take part in enumerations (when using `for...in`, for example).\\n\\n- `configurable`: whether the property can be deleted or its attributes changed.\\n\\n- An accessor descriptor: Defines properties with getters and setters.\\n\\n- `get`: the function that returns the value of the property.\\n\\n- `set`: the function that establishes the value of the property.\\n\\n- `enumerable` and `configurable`: the same as in a data descriptor.\\n\\nAn example of the use of descriptors:\\n\\nDescriptors are useful for controlling the behaviour of objects, especially when working with external libraries or APIs, where it matters to avoid accidental changes in the objects. By means of them you can make an object\'s properties read-only or hide them from iteration loops, for example.\\nBesides that, descriptors help you implement encapsulation through getters and setters:\\n\\nThe use of property descriptors raises the flexibility of work with objects and gives you the ability to control their behaviour precisely.","let person = {}; \\nObject.defineProperty(person, \'name\', {\\n  value: \'Alice\',  \\n  writable: false, // The property cannot be changed  \\n  enumerable: true, // The property will be displayed in enumerations  \\n  configurable: false // The property cannot be deleted or changed \\n}); \\nconsole.log(person.name); // \\"Alice\\" \\nperson.name = \'Bob\'; // An error in strict mode, ignored in non-strict\\n\\nlet user = {\\n  get fullName() {\\n    return `${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`;  \\n  },  \\n  set fullName(name) {\\n    [this.firstName, this.lastName] = name.split(\' \');  \\n  } \\n}; \\nuser.fullName = \'John Doe\'; \\nconsole.log(user.firstName); // \\"John\\" \\nconsole.log(user.lastName); // \\"Doe\\""],[1036,"There are several ways of making an object immutable. One of the most popular is the `Object.freeze()` method, which forbids the addition, deletion and change of an object\'s properties. You can also use `Object.seal()`, which lets you change the existing properties but not add new ones. For deep objects, recursive freezing is applied.","In JavaScript immutable objects matter when data has to be protected from accidental or deliberate changes. Here are several ways of creating such objects:\\n\\n- `Object.freeze()`:\\nThis is the best-known method for creating an immutable object. It prevents any changes to the object — properties cannot be added, deleted or changed. It works only at the first level of the object, however. Nested objects will stay mutable:\\n\\n- `Object.seal()`:\\nThis method forbids the addition and deletion of properties but lets the existing ones be changed. That is useful if you have to prevent a change of the object\'s structure while leaving the ability to edit the values:\\n\\n- Deep freezing:\\nTo make an object and all of its nested objects immutable, you have to apply `Object.freeze()` recursively:\\n\\nThese methods are widely used in applications where it matters to guarantee the immutability of the data — in functional programming, for example, or in architectures such as Redux, for managing an application\'s state.","const obj = { name: \'Alice\' }; \\nObject.freeze(obj); \\nobj.name = \'Bob\'; // An error in strict mode, ignored in non-strict \\nconsole.log(obj.name); // \\"Alice\\"\\n\\nconst obj = { age: 25 }; \\nObject.seal(obj); \\nobj.age = 26; // The change is permitted"],[1037,"An `ArrayBuffer` is an object that holds a fixed quantity of bytes of data in memory. It is used to work with binary data. `Uint32Array` and `Float32Array` are typed arrays, which represent different data types: `Uint32Array` works with unsigned whole numbers, while `Float32Array` works with floating-point numbers. The main difference between them is in the type of data they hold.","In JavaScript, to work with raw binary data the `ArrayBuffer` object and typed arrays are used.\\n`ArrayBuffer`:\\nAn `ArrayBuffer` represents a block of memory containing a fixed quantity of bytes. In itself it provides no methods for working with the data, but it serves as the foundation for typed arrays, which let you work with specific data types such as whole numbers or floating-point numbers:\\n\\nTyped arrays:\\nTyped arrays are arrays tied to a specific data type. For example:\\n\\n- `Uint32Array`: Represents an array of unsigned 32-bit whole numbers. It is used when you need whole numbers without a sign (indexes or identifiers, for example):\\n\\n- `Float32Array`: This is an array of floating-point numbers (32-bit). It is applied for working with fractional numbers — in mathematical calculations or in graphics, for example:\\n\\nThe difference between `Uint32Array` and `Float32Array`:\\n\\n- `Uint32Array` holds unsigned whole numbers (0 and positive numbers). Every element takes up 4 bytes (32 bits).\\n\\n- `Float32Array` holds floating-point numbers. Every element also takes up 4 bytes, but with support for decimal values.\\n\\nTyped arrays and `ArrayBuffer` are useful for working with binary data — when handling files, streams of data, images or video, for example, or when interacting with low-level APIs such as WebGL.","let buffer = new ArrayBuffer(16); // 16 bytes of memory\\n\\nlet uintArray = new Uint32Array(buffer); \\nuintArray[0] = 42; \\nconsole.log(uintArray[0]); // 42\\n\\nlet floatArray = new Float32Array(buffer); \\nfloatArray[0] = 42.5; \\nconsole.log(floatArray[0]); // 42.5"],[1038,"Iterators are objects that implement the `next()` method and return a value on every iteration. Generators are functions that create iterators by means of `function*` and `yield`. Generators let you control the execution of code and return values as they are needed, which makes working with large or infinite sets of data simpler.","Iterators and generators are concepts that let you iterate over values in stages, which is useful when working with sequences of data or with large volumes of information.\\n\\n- Iterators:\\nAn iterator is an object that implements the `next()` method, which returns the next element of the sequence. This method has two properties: `value` (the current value) and `done` (the indication of the end of the sequence). Here is an example of the creation of a simple iterator:\\n\\n- Generators:\\nGenerators are functions that create iterators. Using `function*`, you can return values by means of `yield` and suspend the execution of the function until the next call:\\n\\nThe application of generators:\\n\\n- Generators let you work with infinite sequences while saving memory, since the data is returned as it is needed.\\n\\n- They are useful for asynchronous programming, when you have to handle data in stages — a stream of data from a server, for example.\\n\\nGenerators and iterators make work with sequences of data and with iterations more flexible and controllable.","function createIterator(arr) {\\n  let index = 0;  \\n  return {  \\n    next: function() {  \\n      return index < arr.length        \\n      ? { value: arr[index++], done: false }        \\n      : { value: undefined, done: true };    \\n    }  \\n  }; \\n} \\nconst iterator = createIterator([1, 2, 3]); \\nconsole.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 1, done: false } \\nconsole.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 2, done: false } \\nconsole.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 3, done: false } \\nconsole.log(iterator.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }\\n\\nfunction* simpleGenerator() {\\n  yield 1;  \\n  yield 2;  \\n  yield 3; \\n} \\nconst generator = simpleGenerator(); \\nconsole.log(generator.next()); // { value: 1, done: false } \\nconsole.log(generator.next()); // { value: 2, done: false } \\nconsole.log(generator.next()); // { value: 3, done: false } \\nconsole.log(generator.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }"],[1039,"`Symbol` is a unique, immutable data type that is used as an identifier for the properties of objects. Every `Symbol` value is guaranteed to be unique, even if their values coincide. In practice `Symbol` is applied to create unique keys for objects that will not conflict with other properties, which is useful for protecting data from being overwritten.","`Symbol` is a primitive data type introduced in ECMAScript 6 (ES6). Symbols are created by means of the `Symbol()` function and are always unique, even if two symbols are created with one and the same description:\\n\\nThe main properties of `Symbol`:\\n\\n- Symbols are unique: even if two symbols have the same description, they are not equal to one another.\\n\\n- They can be used as keys for the properties of objects. That is useful when you have to create keys that are guaranteed not to conflict with other keys, even if they have the same names.\\n\\nApplication in practice:\\n\\n- Unique keys for objects: Symbols can be used as unique identifiers for the properties of objects, which prevents possible conflicts with other properties:\\n\\n- Symbols for the description of hidden properties: Symbols can be used to create \\"hidden\\" properties that will not be visible when iterating over an object with loops such as `for...in`. That lets you create properties that will be hidden from most operations:\\n\\n- Built-in symbols: There are also special built-in symbols, such as `Symbol.iterator`, which are used in JavaScript to implement iterators.\\n\\nSymbols are used to create protected keys in libraries, frameworks and in systems where it matters to avoid collisions in an object\'s properties.","const sym1 = Symbol(\'id\'); \\nconst sym2 = Symbol(\'id\'); \\nconsole.log(sym1 === sym2); // false\\n\\nconst myObj = {}; \\nconst sym = Symbol(\'uniqueKey\'); \\nmyObj[sym] = \'Symbol value\'; \\nconsole.log(myObj[sym]); // \'Symbol value\'\\n\\nconst hidden = Symbol(\'hidden\'); \\nconst obj = {\\n  [hidden]: \'secret value\',  \\n  visible: \'public value\' \\n}; \\nfor (let key in obj) {\\n  console.log(key); // will print only \'visible\' \\n}"],[1040,"The chaining of functions is a technique that lets you call several methods in sequence in a single expression. That is achieved by every method returning the object itself on which it is called. An example can be the calling of the methods of arrays such as `map()`, `filter()` and `reduce()`, or the building of objects through successive calls of methods.","The chaining of functions (or method chaining) is a convenient way of simplifying the calls of methods when several actions are carried out in sequence. The principle consists in every method of an object returning the object itself, which lets you call the next method straight away.\\nAn example:\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- In the example above every method (`add`, `subtract`, `multiply`) returns the current object (`this`), which lets you call the following methods in a chain.\\n\\n- Such an approach makes the code more compact and easily readable.\\n\\nThe chaining of functions in practice:\\nWork with arrays: The methods of arrays, such as `map()`, `filter()` and `reduce()`, are often used together thanks to chaining:\\n\\nChaining improves the readability of code and lets developers avoid superfluous intermediate variables, making the logic more linear and intuitive.","class Calculator {\\n  constructor(value = 0) {  \\n    this.value = value;  \\n  }  \\n  add(num) {\\n    this.value += num;    \\n    return this; // we return the object for the chaining  \\n  }  \\n  subtract(num) {  \\n    this.value -= num;    \\n    return this; // we return the object for the chaining  \\n  }  \\n  multiply(num) {  \\n    this.value *= num;    \\n    return this;  \\n  }  \\n  getResult() {\\n    return this.value;  \\n  } \\n} \\nconst calc = new Calculator(); \\nconst result = calc.add(5).subtract(2).multiply(3).getResult(); // the chaining of the methods \\nconsole.log(result); // 9\\n\\nconst result = [1, 2, 3, 4]  \\n  .map(num => num * 2)  \\n  .filter(num => num > 4)  \\n  .reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0); \\nconsole.log(result); // 14"],[1041,"The `mouseleave` event fires when the mouse cursor goes beyond the bounds of an element, its child elements included. The `mouseout` event, for its part, fires when the cursor leaves an element or its child element. `mouseout` can therefore fire several times inside a single element when moving between child elements.","The `mouseleave` and `mouseout` events in JavaScript are connected with the movement of the mouse cursor, but they have different triggers.\\n`mouseleave`:\\nThis event fires when the cursor goes beyond the bounds of an element and all of its child elements. It does not fire if the cursor moves between child elements:\\n\\n`mouseout`:\\nThis event fires when the cursor leaves an element or any of its child elements. Therefore, if the cursor moves between child elements, `mouseout` will fire every time:\\n\\nAn example of the difference:\\n\\nIn this example, if the cursor moves from the parent element onto the child one, `mouseout` on the parent and on the child element will fire, but `mouseleave` will not fire until the cursor has left the parent element entirely.\\nSummary:\\n\\n- `mouseleave` is convenient when you have to track the complete leaving of an element, its child elements included.\\n\\n- `mouseout` fires on every exit of the cursor from an element or its child elements, which makes it useful for tracking every movement between elements inside a structure.","element.addEventListener(\'mouseleave\', () => {\\n  console.log(\'The cursor has left the element\'); \\n});\\n\\nelement.addEventListener(\'mouseout\', () => {\\n  console.log(\'The cursor has left the element or its child element\'); \\n});\\n\\n<div id=\\"parent\\" style=\\"width:200px; height:200px; background-color:lightblue;\\">\\n  <div id=\\"child\\" style=\\"width:100px; height:100px; background-color:lightgreen;\\"></div> \\n</div> \\n<script>\\n  const parent = document.getElementById(\'parent\');  \\n  parent.addEventListener(\'mouseleave\', () => console.log(\'mouseleave on the parent\'));  \\n  parent.addEventListener(\'mouseout\', () => console.log(\'mouseout on the parent\'));  \\n  const child = document.getElementById(\'child\');  \\n  child.addEventListener(\'mouseout\', () => console.log(\'mouseout on the child element\')); \\n<\/script>"],[1042,"The optimisation of the performance of web pages includes several methods: the minimisation of the size of files (the compression of images, the use of minified code), the reduction of the number of HTTP requests (the use of the combining of files and of caching), the postponed loading of resources (lazy loading), and also the optimisation of work with the DOM and the rendering.\\nAll of these approaches are aimed at improving a page\'s loading time and raising the interface\'s responsiveness.","To improve the performance of web pages there are a multitude of approaches, which help speed up the loading and the rendering of the content, which ultimately improves the user experience. Here are several of the main strategies:\\n\\n- The minimisation and compression of files:\\nThe reduction of the size of JavaScript, CSS and image files by means of minification (the removal of spaces and comments and the shortening of the names of variables) and compression (the use of formats such as gzip or Brotli) lowers the loading time of a page.\\n\\n- Lazy Loading:\\nThe postponed loading of images and other heavy resources lets you load only those elements that are necessary for the current screen, which reduces the volume of data loaded on the initial render of the page.\\n\\n- Caching:\\nThe use of HTTP caching lets browsers save static resources (such as images and styles) and reuse them on subsequent visits to the site, reducing the number of requests to the server.\\n\\n- The asynchronous loading of resources:\\nScripts and styles can be loaded asynchronously by means of the `async` and `defer` attributes for `<script>`, which lets pages be loaded faster and avoids the blocking of the rendering.\\n\\n- The reduction of the number of HTTP requests:\\nThe combining of CSS and JavaScript files (bundling) lowers the number of requests necessary to load a page.\\n\\n- The optimisation of work with the DOM:\\nBringing in minimal changes to the DOM and avoiding frequent re-renders helps improve performance, especially when working with large pages.\\n\\n- The use of a CDN (Content Delivery Network):\\nA CDN helps deliver content faster thanks to the files being held on servers located closer to the users.\\n\\nAn example of code with asynchronous loading:\\n\\nThe optimisation of performance is an important aspect of development, since slow pages can lead to a worsening of the user experience and a reduction in a site\'s traffic.","<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script> <!-- The script will load asynchronously -->"],[1043,"To optimise resource-intensive operations you can use `debounce` and `throttle` to cut down the frequency of calls of functions, `requestAnimationFrame` to optimise the rendering of animations, and Web Workers to carry out heavy computations in a background thread.\\nThese methods help lower the load on the main thread and prevent the freezing of the interface.","The optimisation of resource-intensive operations in JavaScript is a key point for maintaining a smooth user interface. The browser\'s main thread is responsible for the rendering of the content, and if long computations take place in it, that can lead to the freezing of the interface. Here are several methods for solving such problems:\\n\\n- Debounce and Throttle:\\nThese techniques are used to optimise frequently called functions (on scrolling or the input of text, for example). `Debounce` guarantees that a function will be called only once after the stream of events has ceased for a particular time. `Throttle` limits the number of calls of a function within a given interval of time.\\nAn example of debounce:\\n\\n- requestAnimationFrame:\\nFor animations and updates of the DOM it is recommended that you use `requestAnimationFrame`, since this method lets the browser synchronise the updates with its rendering cycle, which ensures smoother execution.\\nAn example of the use:\\n\\n- Web Workers:\\nWeb Workers let you carry out heavy computations in a separate thread without blocking the main rendering thread. That is especially useful for operations with large volumes of data.\\nAn example of the use of a Web Worker:\\n\\n- Memoization:\\nThe remembering of the results of previously carried out functions (memoization) can substantially cut down the number of repeated computations and raise performance.\\n\\nThese approaches help manage heavy operations and ensure smooth interaction with the interface, especially on devices with limited resources.","function debounce(func, delay) {\\n  let timeoutId;  \\n  return function(...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);    \\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => func.apply(this, args), delay);  \\n  }; \\n} \\nwindow.addEventListener(\'resize\', debounce(() => {\\n  console.log(\'Resize event!\'); \\n}, 200));\\n\\nfunction animate() {\\n  // the update of the animation  \\n  requestAnimationFrame(animate); \\n} \\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\'); \\nworker.postMessage(\'Start heavy calculation\'); \\nworker.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'Result from worker:\', event.data); \\n};"],[1044,"Web Accessibility means that websites and applications should be available for use by people with various limitations, such as impairments of sight, hearing or mobility. This matters, since it makes the internet accessible for all users, including those who use special devices such as screen readers.","Web Accessibility is the concept of creating websites and applications that can be used by people with various limitations, including impairments of sight, hearing, mobility and cognitive functions. The principles of web accessibility include the adaptation of interfaces for work with assistive technologies, such as screen readers, and ensuring that every element of a web page is reachable from the keyboard.\\nWhy does this matter?\\n\\n- Inclusivity: Around 15 % of the world\'s population have some form of disability. Ensuring the accessibility of websites makes the internet more inclusive for all users.\\n\\n- Legislation: In many countries there are laws and standards (WCAG, for example) that require the principles of web accessibility to be observed. The non-observance of these standards can lead to legal consequences.\\n\\n- Better performance: The creation of an accessible site often leads to an improvement in the overall performance and the user experience, since such sites are, as a rule, better structured and more convenient to use.\\n\\nThe main practices of accessibility:\\n\\n- Alternative text for images: The addition of `alt` text to images, so that screen readers can describe them to people with impaired sight.\\n\\n- Focusable elements: Make sure that every interactive element is reachable through the keyboard (buttons and links, for example).\\n\\n- The contrast of colours: The use of sufficient contrast between the text and the background to improve legibility.\\n\\nAn example of a simple improvement in accessibility:\\n\\nAccessibility on the internet makes it convenient for all users, people with limitations included, and contributes to the creation of better and more inclusive products.","<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A description of the image\\">"],[1045,"The key metrics for assessing the performance of a website include First Contentful Paint (FCP), which measures when content is first displayed; Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), which measures when the page\'s main content becomes visible; First Input Delay (FID), which shows how fast the site reacts to the user\'s first interaction; and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS), which measures the stability of the page\'s layout. These metrics help you assess how fast and how stably a website loads.","To assess the performance of a website developers use several key metrics, which help measure how fast and how efficiently a page loads and reacts to the user\'s actions. Here are the main metrics that are most often applied:\\n\\n- First Contentful Paint (FCP): FCP measures the time when the browser begins to render the first element on the page (text, an image, a block). This matters, since it shows when the user sees the first visual changes on the page. The faster the FCP, the better the user perceives the loading of the site.\\n\\n- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP): LCP measures the time within which the page\'s main content becomes visible (a large image or text, for example). A good LCP is an indicator of the fast loading of important content. The metric helps you understand how long the user has to wait before the main information appears.\\n\\n- First Input Delay (FID): FID measures the time from the user\'s first interaction (the pressing of a button, for example) to the moment when the browser reacts to that action. A high FID can be caused by heavy JavaScript operations blocking the interface. An improvement in FID makes a site more responsive.\\n\\n- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS): CLS measures the stability of the page\'s layout while it is loading. If elements unexpectedly move during the loading (because of the late loading of fonts or images, for example), that causes a negative experience for the user. A good CLS shows that the page does not \\"jump\\" during the rendering.\\n\\nAn example of values of CLS:\\n\\nThese metrics make up the foundation for assessing the user experience and serve as the reference points for improving a site\'s performance, which makes it faster and more convenient for users.","<style>\\n  img { width: 100%; height: auto; } /* Specifying the dimensions prevents the jumps */ \\n</style>"],[1046,"JavaScript supports several programming paradigms: imperative, object-oriented and functional programming. In the imperative style the developer describes step by step what the code should do. Object-oriented programming (OOP) is founded on the creation of objects and classes, while functional programming is built on the use of functions as first-class values, pure functions and the avoidance of changes of state.","JavaScript is a multi-paradigm language, which means that it supports several styles of programming. That makes it very flexible and suitable for solving different tasks. Let us look at the main programming paradigms that are supported in JavaScript:\\n\\n- Imperative programming:\\nThis is a style of programming in which the developer explicitly describes the steps that the computer has to carry out to achieve the result. In JavaScript this is expressed through ordinary statements, loops and conditions.\\n\\n- Object-oriented programming (OOP):\\nIn JavaScript you can create objects and classes to represent entities with state (properties) and behaviour (methods). JavaScript supports prototypal inheritance, which lets objects inherit properties and methods from one another. OOP helps organise code in the form of separate objects, which makes it better structured and easily extensible.\\n\\n- Functional programming (FP):\\nJavaScript also supports functional programming, which is built on the use of functions as first-class values (functions can be passed as arguments and returned as values), the creation of pure functions (without side effects) and the avoidance of changes of state. FP makes code more predictable and easier to test.\\n\\nIn addition:\\n\\n- JavaScript also supports reactive programming through libraries such as RxJS, where the handling of the data is built on streams.\\n\\n- Asynchronous programming, using callbacks, promises and `async/await`, lets you write code that efficiently handles asynchronous tasks.\\n\\nJavaScript is a flexible language, which lets developers combine different approaches to solve tasks, making code more adaptable and maintainable.","for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\\n  console.log(i); // The imperative execution of a loop \\n}\\n\\nclass Car {\\n  constructor(model, year) {\\n    this.model = model;    \\n    this.year = year;  \\n  }    \\n  drive() {\\n    console.log(`The ${this.model} is driving.`);  \\n  } \\n} \\nconst myCar = new Car(\'Toyota\', 2020); \\nmyCar.drive(); // The Toyota is driving.\\n\\nconst add = (x, y) => x + y; // A pure function \\nconst double = x => x * 2; \\nconst result = [1, 2, 3].map(double); // An example of functional handling \\nconsole.log(result); // [2, 4, 6]"],[1047,"The imperative approach in programming focuses on how exactly a task has to be carried out — step by step, with every instruction specified. In the declarative approach you describe what has to be achieved without specifying the precise steps of the execution.\\nThe imperative style is usually used for loops and statements, and the declarative one for work with functions — through methods of arrays such as `map` and `filter`, for example.","The imperative and the declarative approaches are two different styles of programming, which differ in how the developer describes the carrying out of tasks.\\n\\n- The imperative approach:\\nWith imperative programming you specify step-by-step instructions on how a task has to be carried out. You describe every action in detail, and the code manages the process of solving the task. This style is oriented towards changing the program\'s state through specific steps.\\n\\nIn this example the `for` loop explicitly specifies what has to be done at every step: iterate over the array, double the values and add them to a new array. You control every step of the execution.\\n\\n- The declarative approach:\\nIn declarative programming you describe what has to be obtained without going into the details of how that is achieved. You do not specify the steps of the task\'s execution but entrust that to the internal logic of the language or of a library. This style is usually more concise and higher-level, letting you concentrate on the result rather than on the process of the execution.\\n\\nIn this example the array method `map` is used, which carries out the same task (the doubling of the elements) but does it declaratively. We do not describe how the iteration over the array proceeds — we simply specify that every element has to be multiplied by 2.\\n\\nBoth approaches have their pluses and minuses. The imperative style is good for low-level control, while the declarative one makes code more readable and maintainable, especially in complex applications.","// The imperative solution of the task of doubling an array\'s numbers \\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst doubledNumbers = []; \\nfor (let i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {\\n  doubledNumbers.push(numbers[i] * 2); \\n} \\nconsole.log(doubledNumbers); // [2, 4, 6, 8]\\n\\n// The declarative solution of the same task \\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst doubledNumbers = numbers.map(num => num * 2); \\nconsole.log(doubledNumbers); // [2, 4, 6, 8]"],[1048,"CSS Houdini is a set of APIs that lets developers interact with the browser\'s rendering engine and create their own CSS effects and properties. Houdini gives you the ability to write JavaScript in order to extend the functionality of CSS, adding new capabilities for styling.","CSS Houdini is a technology that provides access to the browser\'s internal processes, letting you create and apply your own styles and effects at the stage of the compilation of the styles.\\nHoudini includes several APIs, such as the Paint API (the creation of your own drawings and backgrounds), the Layout API (the definition of your own layout schemes) and the Typed OM (access to the object model of the styles).\\nFor example, by means of the Paint API you can create a background generated dynamically on the basis of data, while the Layout API lets you specify unique layout behaviour. This makes styles more powerful and more configurable.",null],[1049,"Critical CSS is the set of styles necessary for the initial display of a page, which are embedded in the HTML in order to avoid a delay in the loading. The remaining styles are loaded asynchronously. The lazy loading of styles includes the use of JavaScript to attach or load CSS dynamically on a condition, which lets styles be loaded as they are needed.","Critical CSS includes the styles necessary for the display of the content that is \\"above the fold\\", and is embedded directly in the HTML document in order to speed the first paint up.\\nAfter that the remaining styles can be loaded asynchronously, using the `rel=\\"preload\\"` attribute or `media=\\"print\\"` with a subsequent change to `media=\\"all\\"`, in order to postpone their application until the page has loaded. This prevents the blocking of the paint of the main page, and after the complete loading lets the styles be made active for all devices.\\nThe lazy loading of styles lets you attach CSS files as they are needed: by means of JavaScript to load styles when scrolling to a particular section, for example, or using the `media` attribute to attach styles when a given condition is met.\\nYou can also apply bundlers and tools such as `Webpack` and `critical`, which automatically single out the critical styles and simplify the process of the optimisation.\\nAn example of the use of lazy loading:\\n\\nIn this example the styles are loaded asynchronously, since `media=\\"print\\"` initially prevents their application to the display on screen. Once loaded, the attribute is changed to `media=\\"all\\"`, and the styles begin to apply to every type of device.","<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\" media=\\"print\\" onload=\\"this.media=\'all\'\\">"],[1050,"`Object.entries()` returns an array in which every element is an array of two elements: the key and the value of the corresponding property of the object. This is useful for work with objects when both the keys and the values have to be handled — for iterating over them or transforming them, for example.","The `Object.entries()` method in JavaScript transforms an object into an array of key-value pairs. Every element of the array is a two-element array: the first element is the key (a string) and the second is the value tied to that key. The method works only with an object\'s own enumerable properties, excluding inherited properties and symbols.\\nAn example:\\n\\nHere `Object.entries(person)` returns an array of arrays, in which every pair represents a key and the corresponding value of the object. That makes the method useful for cases in which you have to work with an object as with an array of data.\\n`Object.entries()` is convenient to use together with the methods for working with arrays, such as `forEach`, `map` or `reduce`. By means of it you can easily iterate over an object and print the keys and the values, for example:\\n\\nThis method is also applied when sorting objects or transforming them into other structures such as a Map, or for more complex manipulation of data.\\nAn example of the use for the conversion of an object into a Map:\\n\\nThe `Object.entries()` method provides a flexible way of working with objects in the form of arrays, which is especially useful when writing functions in which it matters to interact with an object\'s keys and values at the same time.","const person = {\\n  name: \'Alice\',\\n  age: 25,\\n  profession: \'Engineer\'\\n};\\nconsole.log(Object.entries(person));\\n// [[\'name\', \'Alice\'], [\'age\', 25], [\'profession\', \'Engineer\']]\\n\\nObject.entries(person).forEach(([key, value]) => {\\n  console.log(`${key}: ${value}`);\\n});\\n// The output:\\n// name: Alice\\n// age: 25\\n// profession: Engineer\\n\\nconst personMap = new Map(Object.entries(person));\\nconsole.log(personMap.get(\'name\')); // \'Alice\'"],[1051,"JSON data can be sent from JavaScript by means of the `fetch` method or `axios`. FastAPI automatically parses the JSON body of the request and passes the data to the route\'s handler.","To send JSON data from a front end to FastAPI the `fetch` method is used. FastAPI supports the automatic conversion of JSON into Python objects when type annotations are specified for the parameters of the route\'s function.\\nAn example on the front-end side (JavaScript):\\n\\nAn example of a FastAPI route:\\n\\nWhen the front end sends the data, FastAPI automatically converts the JSON into a `User` object and carries out the validation.\\nWhen to use it:\\n\\n- For client-server applications in which the front end sends structured data to the server.","const data = { name: \\"John\\", age: 30 };\\n\\nfetch(\\"http://localhost:8000/user/\\", {\\n    method: \\"POST\\",\\n    headers: {\\n        \\"Content-Type\\": \\"application/json\\",\\n    },\\n    body: JSON.stringify(data),\\n})\\n    .then((response) => response.json())\\n    .then((result) => console.log(result));\\n\\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\\n\\napp = FastAPI()\\n\\nclass User(BaseModel):\\n      name: str\\n      age: int\\n\\n@app.post(\\"/user/\\")\\ndef create_user(user: User):\\n      return {\\"message\\": f\\"User {user.name} is {user.age} years old\\"}"],[1052,"AJAX in PHP is implemented by means of JavaScript, which sends asynchronous requests to the server through the `XMLHttpRequest` object. On the server a PHP script handles the request, carrying out the necessary operations (access to a database, for example), and sends the response in JSON or XML format. JavaScript then handles that response and updates the web page without a reload.","AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML) lets you update part of a web page without the need to reload the whole document. In PHP this is implemented through interaction with the client side using JavaScript and server-side handling through PHP.\\nJavaScript sends the request: In JavaScript an `XMLHttpRequest` object is created, which sends an asynchronous request to the server. This request can be a GET or a POST, depending on the need to pass data.\\nAn example in JavaScript:\\n\\nPHP handles the request: On the server PHP receives the data, carries out the necessary operations (turns to a database, for example) and returns the result in a format that JavaScript can handle, usually JSON.\\nAn example in PHP (server.php):\\n\\nJavaScript updates the page: After receiving the data, JavaScript can dynamically update the page, inserting the data received into the necessary place.\\nAJAX lets you create more dynamic and more responsive web applications, minimising the need for a reload of the page and improving the user experience.","function sendRequest() {\\n      var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();\\n      xhr.open(\\"GET\\", \\"server.php?param=value\\", true);\\n      xhr.onreadystatechange = function() {\\n          if (xhr.readyState == 4 && xhr.status == 200) {\\n                document.getElementById(\\"response\\").innerHTML = xhr.responseText;\\n          }\\n      };\\n      xhr.send();\\n}\\n\\nif (isset($_GET[\'param\'])) {\\n      $response = [\\"message\\" => \\"Data received: \\" . $_GET[\'param\']];\\n      echo json_encode($response);\\n}"],[1053,"PHP interacts with JavaScript by generating JavaScript code, which is then carried out on the client side. PHP can pass data into JavaScript through the HTML or create asynchronous requests by means of AJAX, letting pages be updated dynamically without a reload.","PHP and JavaScript work in different parts of a web application: PHP on the server and JavaScript on the client. They can interact, however, and that happens in the following way:\\nThe generation of JavaScript from PHP: PHP can generate JavaScript that will be carried out on the client side. An example:\\n\\nThe use of AJAX: PHP handles on the server the data obtained by means of JavaScript (through AJAX requests, for example) and sends responses, which can be used to update part of the page. An example:\\n\\nThe interaction of PHP and JavaScript lets you create dynamic and interactive web pages, in which the server logic and the client-side dynamics work in concert.","$name = \\"John\\";\\necho \\"<script>var username = \'$name\'; alert(username);<\/script>\\";\\n\\nvar xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();\\nxhr.open(\\"GET\\", \\"data.php\\", true);\\nxhr.onreadystatechange = function() {\\n      if (xhr.readyState == 4 && xhr.status == 200) {\\n          document.getElementById(\\"result\\").innerHTML = xhr.responseText;\\n      }\\n};\\nxhr.send();"],[1054,"If in the array `[1, 2, 3]` you write a value at index `7` — `arr[7] = 10`, for example — its length will become `8`. JavaScript automatically extends the array, filling the skipped indexes with \\"empty slots\\" (not `undefined`, but similar).","Arrays in JavaScript are dynamic, and their length changes when you write beyond the bounds of the current size. For example:\\n\\nIndexes `3`–`6` thereby become \\"empty slots\\" (`empty`) but not `undefined`. If you try to read them, `undefined` will be returned, but the array itself is not filled with explicit values.","const arr = [1, 2, 3];\\narr[7] = 10;\\nconsole.log(arr.length); // 8"],[1055,"Use the `typeof` operator:\\n\\n`if (typeof myVar === \'function\') { ... } `\\nThis is a safe way, which works even for `null` and `undefined`.","In JavaScript functions are objects of a particular type, and `typeof` returns the string `\'function\'` for them. An example:\\n\\nOther ways (`instanceof Function`, for example) also work, but they can give unexpected results in some contexts (between frames in a browser, for example).","function greet() { console.log(\'Hello!\'); }\\nconst arrowFn = () => {};\\nconst notFn = 42;\\n\\nconsole.log(typeof greet);    // \'function\'\\nconsole.log(typeof arrowFn);  // \'function\'\\nconsole.log(typeof notFn);    // \'number\'"],[1056,"Use `Array.isArray()`:\\n\\n`if (Array.isArray(myVar)) { ... } `\\nThis is the most reliable way, and it works in every modern browser and in Node.js.","Arrays in JavaScript are a particular type of object, and `typeof []` will return `\'object\'`, which does not help you distinguish them from ordinary objects. Previously the hack `myVar instanceof Array` was used, but it is unreliable in some cases (when working with an iframe, for example).\\nThe `Array.isArray()` method solves this problem:\\n\\nThis way is supported by every modern JavaScript engine.","const arr = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst obj = {};\\n\\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(arr)); // true\\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(obj)); // false"],[1057,"- Cookies:\\n\\n- A small size (~4 KB per domain).\\n\\n- They are automatically sent to the server with every HTTP request.\\n\\n- They can be persistent (the term expires manually) or session ones (until the browser is closed).\\n\\n- They are used for authorisation and tracking.\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- A larger size (~5 MB per domain).\\n\\n- The data is available only in the current tab and is deleted after it is closed.\\n\\n- It is not sent to the server automatically.\\n\\n- It is used for temporary data (the saving of a form, for example).","- The lifetime\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- If the `expires` or `max-age` parameter is specified, cookies are saved even after the browser is closed.\\n\\n- Without them they are deleted when the session ends (but the behaviour depends on the browser).\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- The data lives only while the tab is open. When it is closed, the data is cleared.\\n\\n- The volume of data\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- A limit of ~4 KB for the whole domain (all cookies in total).\\n\\n- An overflow leads to the loss of the old cookies.\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- Up to ~5 MB per domain (it depends on the browser).\\n\\n- Complex data can be held (JSON objects, for example).\\n\\n- Availability\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- They are automatically attached to every HTTP request in the `Cookie` header.\\n\\n- The server can read or change them through `Set-Cookie`.\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- It is available only through JavaScript on the client.\\n\\n- It is not sent to the server without an explicit instruction (through `fetch`, for example).\\n\\n- The use\\n\\n- When to choose cookies:\\n\\n- For authentication (JWT, session IDs).\\n\\n- For tracking (analytics, personalisation).\\n\\n- When to choose `sessionStorage`:\\n\\n- Temporary data within the bounds of a single tab (the saving of a form before it is sent, for example).\\n\\n- The caching of heavy data in order to avoid repeated computations.\\n\\n- Security\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- They are vulnerable to CSRF attacks (if there is no `SameSite` and `HttpOnly`).\\n\\n- They can be protected:\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- It is not vulnerable to CSRF (since it is not sent automatically).\\n\\n- But it is vulnerable to XSS (if an attacker carries out someone else\'s JS code).","// A cookie with a term of validity (it will be deleted in 7 days)\\ndocument.cookie = \\"token=abc123; max-age=604800; path=/\\";\\n\\n// An HTTP header from the server\\nSet-Cookie: sessionId=xyz; Secure; HttpOnly\\n\\n// An example of the reading of cookies\\nconsole.log(document.cookie); // \\"token=abc123; theme=dark\\"\\n\\n// The saving of an object\\nsessionStorage.setItem(\'formData\', JSON.stringify({ name: \'Alice\', age: 25 }));\\n// The reading\\nconst data = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem(\'formData\'));\\n\\ndocument.cookie = \\"auth=secret; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict\\";"],[1058,"To embed web pages an iframe (an inline frame) is used. This is an HTML element that creates a separate window inside a page for showing another document. You can also use the components of front-end frameworks or server-side includes (SSI), but an iframe is the simplest and most universal way.","An iframe (Inline Frame) is an HTML element that lets you embed one HTML page in another. It creates an independent viewing window inside the parent page\\nThe main attributes of an iframe: src (the address of the page being embedded), width and height (the dimensions), frameborder (the borders)\\nAn example of an iframe:\\n\\nThe advantages of an iframe: the complete isolation of the styles and the scripts, simplicity of use, support for cross-domain resources\\nThe disadvantages: problems with SEO, possible problems with security, the difficulty of managing the dimensions of the content\\nThe alternatives: React/Vue/Angular components for the reuse of UI, the AJAX loading of content, Server Side Includes (SSI) for static sites","<iframe src=\\"https://example.com\\" width=\\"600\\" height=\\"400\\"></iframe>"],[1059,"Lazy loading is used. For that the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute is added to the `<img>` or the Intersection Observer is used in JavaScript.","The HTML attribute `loading=\\"lazy\\"` (supported in modern browsers):\\n\\nThe browser will itself load the image when it appears in the zone of visibility.\\nThe Intersection Observer API (if you need greater control):\\n\\nIn the HTML markup:","<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"An example\\">\\n\\nconst img = document.querySelector(\'img.lazy\');\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;\\n      observer.unobserve(entry.target);\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe(img);\\n\\n<img data-src=\\"real-image.jpg\\" class=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Lazy loading\\">"],[1060,"Debouncing or throttling is used. A debounce waits until the user has stopped typing (300 ms, for example) and only then sends the request.","- Debounce — sends a request only after a pause in the input:\\n\\n- Throttle — limits the frequency of the requests (no more often than once in 500 ms, for example):\\n\\nThe difference:\\n\\n- Debounce is better for autocomplete (it waits for the end of the input).\\n\\n- Throttle is useful on scrolling or resize (it guarantees periodic calls).\\n\\nAn example with Lodash (a library with ready-made functions):","function debounce(func, delay) {\\n  let timeout;\\n  return function() {\\n    clearTimeout(timeout);\\n    timeout = setTimeout(() => func.apply(this, arguments), delay);\\n  };\\n}\\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById(\'search\');\\nsearchInput.addEventListener(\'input\', debounce(function() {\\n  fetchResults(this.value); // The request to the server\\n}, 300));\\n\\nfunction throttle(func, limit) {\\n  let inThrottle;\\n  return function() {\\n    if (!inThrottle) {\\n      func.apply(this, arguments);\\n      inThrottle = true;\\n      setTimeout(() => inThrottle = false, limit);\\n    }\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nimport { debounce } from \'lodash\';\\nsearchInput.addEventListener(\'input\', debounce(fetchResults, 300));"],[1061,"`forEach` simply iterates over an array and carries out a function for every element, without returning a new array. `map` also iterates over an array, but creates and returns a new array with the results of the call of the function for every element.","Both methods are used to iterate over an array, but they work differently.\\n\\n- `forEach` carries out the function passed to it for every element but does not return a new array. It is suitable when you simply have to do something with every element (print it to the console or change the original array, for example).\\n\\n- `map` creates a new array, applying a function to every element. It is useful when you have to transform the data without changing the original array.\\n\\nUse `forEach` for side effects (logging, mutation) and `map` for the creation of a new array on the basis of the old one.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nnumbers.forEach(num => console.log(num * 2)); // Will print: 2, 4, 6\\n// numbers will remain [1, 2, 3]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2);\\nconsole.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6]\\n// numbers will remain [1, 2, 3]"],[1062,"Generators are functions that can be suspended and resumed. They return a special object (a `Generator`), managed through `yield`. They are useful for work with sequences or with asynchronous operations.","Generators are declared with `function*` and use the `yield` keyword to return values step by step.\\nAn example of a simple generator:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- Generators preserve their state between calls of `next()`.\\n\\n- `yield` suspends the execution, and `next()` resumes it.\\n\\n- They can be used for handling data in parts or together with `for...of`.\\n\\nThe practical application:\\n\\n- Lazy computations (the generation of values on request).\\n\\n- Asynchronous operations (in Redux-Saga, for example).","function* countToThree() {\\n  yield 1;\\n  yield 2;\\n  yield 3;\\n}\\nconst generator = countToThree();\\nconsole.log(generator.next().value); // 1\\nconsole.log(generator.next().value); // 2\\nconsole.log(generator.next().value); // 3\\nconsole.log(generator.next().done);  // true"],[1063,"LocalStorage cannot be completely protected from being read or changed by third-party JS, since it is available in one and the same context. The risks can be lowered: hold only non-sensitive data, and use encryption or HttpOnly cookies for critical information.","LocalStorage is available to every script on the same page, so malicious code (an XSS attack, for example) can read or change its data. There is no complete protection, but the threats can be reduced:\\n\\n- Do not hold confidential data (tokens, passwords) in LocalStorage.\\n\\n- Encrypt the data before writing it (by means of `CryptoJS`, for example).\\n\\n- Use HttpOnly cookies for critical data — they are not available from JavaScript.\\n\\n- A Content Security Policy (CSP) — it limits the execution of third-party scripts.\\n\\nAn example of encryption:","// The writing\\nconst data = { user: \\"Alice\\" };\\nconst encrypted = btoa(JSON.stringify(data)); // Base64 (not secure, AES is better)\\nlocalStorage.setItem(\\"safeData\\", encrypted);\\n// The reading\\nconst stored = localStorage.getItem(\\"safeData\\");\\nconst decrypted = JSON.parse(atob(stored));\\nconsole.log(decrypted.user); // \\"Alice\\""],[1064,"Both methods search for an element in a string or an array. `indexOf()` returns the first occurrence of the element (from the beginning), while `lastIndexOf()` returns the last one (from the end). If the element is not found, they return `-1`.","The `indexOf()` and `lastIndexOf()` methods work with strings and arrays:\\n\\n- `indexOf(item)` — searches for `item` from the beginning of the collection and returns its position (the index). For example:\\n\\n- `lastIndexOf(item)` — searches for `item` from the end. An example:\\n\\nBoth methods return `-1` if the element is not found.\\nYou can specify a starting index for the search:","const str = \\"Hello\\";\\nconsole.log(str.indexOf(\\"l\\")); // 2 (the first \\"l\\")\\n\\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30, 20];\\nconsole.log(arr.lastIndexOf(20)); // 3 (the last 20)\\n\\n\\"hello world\\".indexOf(\\"o\\", 5); // searches for \\"o\\" starting from the 5th character -> 7"],[1065,"- A Function Declaration is created before the code is carried out and can be called before the declaration.\\n\\n- A Function Expression is created at the moment of the execution and is called only after the declaration.","- A Function Declaration:\\n\\n- The function is \\"lifted\\" (hoisting) to the beginning of the scope.\\n\\n- A Function Expression:\\n\\n- The variable `sayHi` is created when the code is carried out.\\n\\n- It is used for callbacks and anonymous functions.\\n\\nThe key difference:\\n\\n- A Declaration is for the main functions.\\n\\n- An Expression is for when a function is passed as a value (in `setTimeout`, for example).","sayHello(); // Works (will print \\"Hello!\\")\\nfunction sayHello() {\\n  console.log(\\"Hello!\\");\\n}\\n\\nconst sayHi = function() {\\n  console.log(\\"Hi!\\");\\n};\\nsayHi(); // It will work only this way round"],[1066,"The Event Loop manages the execution of asynchronous code.\\n\\n- Microtasks (a Promise, `queueMicrotask`) are carried out right after the current synchronous code.\\n\\n- Macrotasks (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`) are carried out after the microtasks and the render of the page.","JavaScript is single-threaded, but the Event Loop lets it handle asynchronous operations:\\n\\n- Microtasks:\\n\\n- They are carried out before the macrotasks.\\n\\n- Examples:\\n\\n- Macrotasks:\\n\\n- They are carried out after the microtasks.\\n\\n- Examples:\\n\\nThe order of the execution:\\n\\nSummary:\\n\\n- Microtasks are for urgent asynchronous operations (the update of state, for example).\\n\\n- Macrotasks are for less high-priority tasks (animations, logging).","Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"A microtask\\"));\\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log(\\"Another one\\"));\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"A macrotask\\"), 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(\\"Synchronous code\\");\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"A microtask\\"));\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"A macrotask\\"), 0);\\n// The output:\\n// \\"Synchronous code\\" -> \\"A microtask\\" -> \\"A macrotask\\""],[1067,"Web Vitals are a set of key metrics that Google uses to assess a site\'s speed and convenience. The main ones are:\\n\\n- LCP (Largest Contentful Paint) — the loading time of the largest element.\\n\\n- FID (First Input Delay) — the delay before the reaction to the user\'s first action.\\n\\n- CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift) — unexpected shifts of the elements on a page.","Web Vitals help you understand how fast and how smoothly a site works. Google uses them in its search ranking.\\n\\n- LCP measures how fast the user sees the main content (a heading or an image, for example). A good figure is less than 2.5 seconds.\\n\\n- FID shows how fast a site reacts to clicks or presses. The optimal value is less than 100 ms.\\n\\n- CLS assesses the stability of the layout. If elements jump during the loading (because of slowly loading advertising banners, for example), the CLS worsens.\\n\\nAn example of the measurement in JavaScript:","import {getCLS, getFID, getLCP} from \'web-vitals\';  \\n\\ngetCLS(console.log);  \\ngetFID(console.log);  \\ngetLCP(console.log);"],[1068,"Node.js on the front end is mainly applied for:\\n\\n- The building of projects (Webpack, Vite, Rollup).\\n\\n- The running of a local server for development.\\n\\n- The management of dependencies through npm/yarn/pnpm.\\n\\n- The carrying out of scripts (tests, linting, deployment).\\n\\nFront-end developers do not run Node.js in the browser, but they use it in the build tools.","Node.js is a server-side JavaScript platform, but it is actively used on the front end as a development tool. Here are the main scenarios:\\n\\n- The building of a project (Bundling)\\n\\nModern front-end frameworks (React, Vue, Svelte) require a build before they are run in the browser. Tools such as Webpack, Vite and Rollup work on Node.js and:\\n\\n- Combine the JS files into a single bundle.\\n\\n- Handle CSS, images and other resources.\\n\\n- Apply Babel for support for older browsers.\\n\\nAn example of a `package.json` for building React:\\n\\n- A local server for development\\n\\nTools such as `vite`, `webpack-dev-server` or `create-react-app` run a local server on Node.js with:\\n\\n- Hot reloading (HMR).\\n\\n- The proxying of API requests (in order to avoid CORS).\\n\\nThe run:\\n\\n- The management of dependencies (npm/yarn/pnpm)\\n\\nNode.js includes npm (the Node Package Manager) for the installation of libraries:\\n\\n- The running of scripts (tests, linting, deployment)\\n\\n- Tests: Jest, Cypress, Storybook.\\n\\n- Linting: ESLint, Prettier.\\n\\n- Deployment: Scripts for sending code to a host (Netlify, Vercel).\\n\\nAn example of tests:\\n\\n- SSR (Server-Side Rendering)\\n\\nFrameworks such as Next.js, Nuxt and SvelteKit use Node.js for:\\n\\n- The rendering of pages on the server.\\n\\n- The generation of static sites (SSG).\\n\\nAn example of Next.js:\\n\\nNode.js is not carried out in the browser, but it is critically important for:\\n\\n- The building of a project.\\n\\n- The running of the dev server.\\n\\n- The management of packages.\\n\\n- The automation of tasks (tests, deployment).\\n\\n- SSR/SSG in Next.js and its analogues.\\n\\nWithout Node.js the modern front-end stack (React, Vue and so on) will not work.","{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"build\\": \\"vite build\\"  // or \\"webpack\\", \\"rollup -c\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nnpm run dev  # or vite, next dev, astro dev\\n\\nnpm install react axios  # Adds the packages to node_modules\\n\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"test\\": \\"jest\\",\\n    \\"lint\\": \\"eslint .\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The server renders the page before sending it to the browser\\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  const res = await fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\');\\n  return { props: { data: await res.json() } };\\n}"],[1069,"The optimisation of the loading of sites is the set of ways of making pages faster. The main methods are: the compression of images, the minification of CSS/JS, caching, lazy loading and the use of a CDN. The faster a site, the better the user experience and the higher the positions in the search engines.","The optimisation of the loading of sites includes a multitude of techniques aimed at reducing the response time and improving performance.\\n\\n- The compression of images\\nLarge images slow the loading down. Use the WebP or AVIF formats, which ensure high quality at a smaller size. Tools such as Squoosh or ImageOptim help compress images without loss.\\n\\n- The minification of CSS and JavaScript\\nThe removal of spaces and comments and the shortening of code reduce the size of the files. Webpack, Vite or online tools such as UglifyJS are suitable for that.\\n\\n- Caching\\nBrowsers can save static files (CSS, JS, images) so as not to load them again. Configure the `Cache-Control` and `Expires` HTTP headers on the server.\\n\\n- Lazy Load\\nImages and other resources are loaded only when they enter the zone of the screen\'s visibility. In HTML this is done through the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute for `<img>`.\\n\\n- The use of a CDN\\nA Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a network of servers that deliver content to the user from the node nearest to them. This speeds the loading up, especially for a global audience.\\n\\n- The optimisation of fonts\\nAttach only the weights of fonts that you need and use `font-display: swap`, so that the text is displayed straight away even if the font has not yet loaded.\\n\\nAn example of the code for lazy load:\\n\\nA script can replace `src` with `data-src` when the image appears in the viewport.","<img src=\\"placeholder.jpg\\" data-src=\\"real-image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"An example\\">"],[1070,"A static method is a function of a class that is called without the creation of an instance. It belongs to the class itself rather than to an object. For example, `Math.random()` is a static method, since it does not require `new Math()`.","Static methods are used for functionality that does not depend on a specific object. They are defined by means of the `static` keyword.\\nAn example in JavaScript:\\n\\nThe differences from ordinary methods:\\n\\n- The call: A static method is called through the name of the class (`Class.method()`), while an ordinary one is called through an instance (`obj.method()`).\\n\\n- The context: In a static method there is no access to an object\'s `this`, since it works at the level of the class.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Factory methods (like `createGuest` above).\\n\\n- Auxiliary functions — the validation of data, for example.\\n\\n- Utilities, as in `Math` or `Array.isArray()`.\\n\\nImportant:\\nStatic methods cannot turn to the non-static fields of a class, since they exist before objects are created.","class User {\\n  static createGuest() {\\n    return new User(\\"Guest\\");\\n  }\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n}\\n// The call without new:\\nconst guest = User.createGuest();\\nconsole.log(guest.name); // \\"Guest\\""],[1071,"SOLID is five principles of OOP:\\n\\n- Single Responsibility — A class should solve only one task.\\n\\n- Open-Closed — Code should be open for extension but closed for modification.\\n\\n- Liskov Substitution — Subclasses should replace their parent classes without errors.\\n\\n- Interface Segregation — Many small interfaces are better than one large one.\\n\\n- Dependency Inversion — Dependencies should be built on abstractions rather than on concrete classes.","- The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)\\nEvery class should be responsible for only one task. If a class does too much, it is difficult to maintain.\\nAn example of a violation:\\n\\nThe correction:\\n\\n- The Open-Closed Principle (OCP)\\nClasses should be extensible without a change in their source code.\\nAn example:\\n\\n- The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)\\nSubclasses should replace their parent classes without a violation of the program\'s logic.\\nA violation:\\n\\nThe correction:\\n\\n- The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)\\nClients should not depend on methods that they do not use.\\nThe problem:\\n\\nThe solution:\\n\\n- The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)\\nDependencies should be built on abstractions (interfaces) rather than on concrete classes.\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nSOLID helps you write flexible, comprehensible and easily testable code.","class User {\\n  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }\\n  saveToDatabase() { /* ... */ }\\n  sendEmail() { /* ... */ }\\n  generateReport() { /* ... */ }\\n}\\n\\nclass User { /* Only the data */ }\\nclass UserRepository { saveToDatabase() { /* ... */ } }\\nclass EmailService { sendEmail() { /* ... */ } }\\n\\nclass Logger {\\n  log(message) { console.log(message); }\\n}\\n// We extend it without changing Logger\\nclass FileLogger extends Logger {\\n  log(message) { /* the writing to a file */ }\\n}\\n\\nclass Bird {\\n  fly() { /* ... */ }\\n}\\nclass Penguin extends Bird {} // Penguins do not fly!\\n\\nclass Bird {}\\nclass FlyingBird extends Bird { fly() {} }\\nclass Penguin extends Bird {} // Now it is correct\\n\\ninterface Worker {\\n  work(): void;\\n  eat(): void;\\n}\\nclass Robot implements Worker {\\n  eat() {} // A robot does not need to eat!\\n}\\n\\ninterface Workable { work(): void; }\\ninterface Eatable { eat(): void; }\\n\\nclass Robot implements Workable { work() {} }\\n\\ninterface Database {\\n  save(data: string): void;\\n}\\nclass MySQLDatabase implements Database { /* ... */ }\\nclass UserService {\\n  constructor(private db: Database) {} // We depend on the abstraction\\n}"],[1072,"A pure function is a function that:\\n\\n- Always returns the same result for the same arguments.\\n\\n- Has no side effects (it does not change external data).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n`const sum = (a, b) => a + b;`","The criteria of purity\\n\\n- Determinism\\n\\n- With the same input data the result is the same.\\n\\n- An example of an impure function: `Math.random()` (it returns something different each time).\\n\\n- The absence of side effects\\n\\n- It does not change global variables and does not mutate its arguments.\\n\\n- An example of an impure function:\\n\\nExamples\\nA pure function:\\n\\nAn impure function:\\n\\nThe advantages of pure functions\\n\\n- They are simpler to test (no mocks are needed).\\n\\n- They are more comprehensible (there are no hidden dependencies).\\n\\n- They can be cached (memoization, for example).\\n\\nPure functions make code predictable and convenient for refactoring. They are widely used in React (reducers) and in functional programming.","let counter = 0;\\nfunction increment() { counter++; } // It changes the external state\\n\\nfunction multiply(a, b) {\\n  return a * b;\\n}\\n\\nlet tax = 0.2;\\nfunction calculateTotal(price) {\\n  return price * (1 + tax); // It depends on an external variable\\n}"],[1073,"Data structures are the ways of organising and holding information in a program. The main types are:\\n\\n- Arrays — ordered lists of elements.\\n- Objects / hash tables — \\"key-value\\" pairs for fast access.\\n- Linked lists — elements connected by references (convenient for insertion and deletion).\\n- Stacks and queues — collections with LIFO (a stack) and FIFO (a queue) order.\\n- Trees and graphs — hierarchical and network data structures.","Data structures help you hold and handle information efficiently. For example:\\n\\n- Arrays (`[1, 2, 3]`) are simple, but insertion into the middle is slow.\\n- Hash tables (`{ key: value }`) let you find data quickly by a key.\\n- Linked lists save memory and are easily changed, but searching in them is slow.\\n- A stack (like a pile of plates) is used for the undoing of actions (Ctrl+Z), for example.\\n- Trees (like the DOM in a browser) are needed for hierarchical data.\\nEvery structure is useful in different scenarios: arrays for simple lists, hash tables for a fast search, and trees for nested data.",null],[1074,"JavaScript carries code out synchronously (step by step), but it can postpone tasks by means of:\\n\\n- Callback functions (`setTimeout`, for example).\\n- Promises (`Promise`) and `async/await`.\\n- The Event Loop, which handles asynchronous tasks after the main code.","JavaScript is single-threaded, but it avoids blocking by means of asynchronicity. For example:\\n\\nHere `setTimeout` postpones the execution so as not to get in the way of the main thread.\\nHow does this work?\\n\\n- Synchronous code is carried out straight away.\\n\\n- Asynchronous tasks (requests, timers) land in a queue (the Callback Queue).\\n\\n- The Event Loop checks when the main code has finished and then carries out the tasks from the queue.\\n\\nPromises (`Promise`) and `async/await` simplify work with asynchronicity, making code more comprehensible:","console.log(\\"Start\\");\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"Timeout\\"), 0);\\nconsole.log(\\"End\\");\\n// The output: Start -> End -> Timeout\\n\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const response = await fetch(\\"url\\"); // We wait for the response without blocking\\n  console.log(response);\\n}"],[1075,"Yes, it blocks it. A `Promise` does not make code asynchronous in itself — it merely postpones its execution. If there are complex computations inside a promise (a loop, for example), they will be carried out in the main thread and will freeze the interface.","Promises are not multithreading but a way of organising asynchronous code. For example:\\n\\nThis code will block the page until the loop has finished.\\nHow do you avoid the blocking?\\n\\n- Web Workers — the moving of the computations out into a separate thread.\\n\\n- The splitting into parts (chunking) by means of `setTimeout` or `requestIdleCallback`.\\n\\n- The optimisation of the algorithms (caching, for example).\\n\\nAn example with a Web Worker:\\n\\nThis way the computations will not get in the way of the interface\'s working.","new Promise((resolve) => {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {} // A long calculation\\n  resolve(\\"Done\\");\\n}).then(console.log);\\n\\n// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker(\\"worker.js\\");\\nworker.postMessage(1e9); // We send the data to the worker\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => console.log(e.data); // We receive the result\\n// worker.js\\nonmessage = (e) => {\\n  let sum = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < e.data; i++) sum += i;\\n  postMessage(sum); // We send the result back\\n};"],[1076,"So that heavy computations do not block the interface, they can be:\\n\\n- Moved out into a Web Worker — the code is carried out in a background thread.\\n- Split into parts (chunking) by means of `setTimeout` or `requestIdleCallback`.\\n- Handled with WASM (WebAssembly) for high-performance computations.\\n- Optimised at the level of the algorithms — nested loops can be replaced with more efficient methods, for example.","The browser uses one main thread for JS and the render, so long computations (the sorting of large arrays, complex mathematics) can \\"freeze\\" the interface. The solutions:\\n\\n- Web Workers\\nThey let you run code in a separate thread. An example:\\n\\nThe pluses: It does not block the UI.\\nThe minuses: You cannot work with the DOM directly.\\n\\n- The splitting into parts (Chunking)\\nIf Workers are not available, a task can be split into small parts:\\n\\nThe pluses: Simplicity.\\nThe minuses: It is slower than Workers.\\n\\n- WebAssembly (WASM)\\nFor extremely heavy computations (3D graphics or cryptography, for example):\\n\\nThe pluses: The speed is close to that of native code.\\nThe minuses: The complexity of the setup.","// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\');  \\nworker.postMessage(1000000); // We send the data  \\nworker.onmessage = (e) => console.log(e.data); // We receive the result  \\n// worker.js  \\nonmessage = (e) => {  \\n  let sum = 0;  \\n  for (let i = 0; i < e.data; i++) sum += i;  \\n  postMessage(sum); // We send it back  \\n};\\n\\nfunction processChunk(start, end) {  \\n  for (let i = start; i < end; i++) { /* ... */ }  \\n  if (end < 1e6) {  \\n    setTimeout(() => processChunk(end, end + 1000), 0);  \\n  }  \\n}  \\nprocessChunk(0, 1000);\\n\\n// An example with the use of a WASM module  \\nWebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(fetch(\'module.wasm\'))  \\n  .then((obj) => obj.instance.exports.heavyCalculation());"],[1077,"No, a Web Worker cannot change the DOM or render components directly. It works in an isolated thread without access to `document` or `window`. You can, however:\\n\\n- Pass the data to the main thread, where React will update the interface.\\n- Use proxy libraries of the `comlink` kind in order to simplify the exchange of data.","Web Workers carry code out in the background, but they have no access to the DOM because of browsers\' security rules. An example of the way round it:\\nStep 1: The Worker computes the data\\n\\nStep 2: The main thread updates React\\n\\nThe alternatives:\\n\\n- A Service Worker + a Virtual DOM: You can render components in a Worker in advance and pass the markup as a string.\\n\\n- Offscreen rendering: In a Canvas through `OffscreenCanvas`, for example (only for graphics).","// worker.js  \\nonmessage = (e) => {  \\n  const result = heavyCalculation(e.data);  \\n  postMessage({ type: \'UPDATE_UI\', payload: result });  \\n};\\n\\n// main.js  \\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\');  \\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {  \\n  if (e.data.type === \'UPDATE_UI\') {  \\n    setState(e.data.payload); // We update React\'s state  \\n   }  \\n};"],[1078,"`preventDefault()` cancels the browser\'s standard action (it prevents the navigation to a link, for example). `stopPropagation()` stops the bubbling of an event, so that it is not passed to the parent elements. Both methods are used for more flexible management of events.","When an event takes place (a click, for example), the browser carries out standard actions (the navigation to a link on a click on an `<a>`, for example). `preventDefault()` cancels that action.\\nAn example:\\n\\nEvents in the DOM bubble (they are passed from a child element to the parent one). `stopPropagation()` stops that bubbling:\\n\\nThese methods are useful when you have to override the default behaviour or control which elements will receive an event.","document.querySelector(\'a\').addEventListener(\'click\', (e) => {\\n  e.preventDefault(); // The page will not reload\\n});\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener(\'click\', (e) => {\\n   e.stopPropagation(); // The parent div will not receive this event\\n});"],[1079,"A binary search speeds the search for an element in a sorted array up, cutting the time from O(n) to O(log n). It works by comparing the value sought with the middle element and discarding half of the array at every step.","An ordinary iteration over an array (a linear search) has a complexity of O(n), but if the array is sorted, a binary search can be used.\\nThe algorithm:\\n\\n- We find the middle of the array.\\n\\n- If the value sought is equal to the middle element, we return it.\\n\\n- If the value is smaller, we search in the left half; if it is larger, in the right one.\\n\\n- We repeat until we find the element or the array runs out.\\n\\nAn example of the code:\\n\\nA binary search works only with sorted data, but it is far faster than a linear one.","function binarySearch(arr, target) {\\n  let left = 0;\\n  let right = arr.length - 1;\\n  while (left <= right) {\\n    const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);\\n     if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;\\n       if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;\\n      else right = mid - 1;\\n  }\\n  return -1; // The element was not found\\n}"],[1080,"Memoization saves the results of the execution of a function for one and the same arguments in order to avoid repeated computations. This is useful for heavy functions (recursive calculations, for example).","Memoization works like this:\\n\\n- On the first call of the function with some arguments the result is saved in a cache.\\n\\n- On a repeated call with the same arguments the saved result is taken.\\n\\nAn example with the recursive computation of a factorial:\\n\\nIn modern libraries (Lodash, for example) there is a ready-made `_.memoize` function.\\nMemoization is useful for:\\n\\n- Recursive functions (the Fibonacci numbers, for example).\\n\\n- Expensive computations (rendering, complex mathematical operations).\\n\\n- Functions with the same arguments (API requests, for example).\\n\\nBut it requires memory for holding the cache, so it is not always suitable.","const memo = {};\\nfunction factorial(n) {\\n  if (n in memo) return memo[n]; // We return the cached result\\n  if (n <= 1) return 1;\\n  memo[n] = n * factorial(n - 1); // We save the result\\n  return memo[n];\\n}"],[1081,"For the deletion of elements `splice(start, deleteCount)` is used, which changes the original array. For insertion there is `splice(start, 0, ...items)`, where `...items` are the elements to be added. You can also use `slice()` to create a new array without changes to the original.","The deletion of elements\\nThe `splice(start, deleteCount)` method deletes `deleteCount` elements starting from the index `start` and returns the deleted elements.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIf you have to delete an element by value, you can use `filter()` (it does not change the original array):\\n\\nThe insertion of elements\\nThe `splice(start, 0, ...items)` method inserts elements starting from the position `start`:\\n\\nThe replacement of elements\\nYou can delete and insert at the same time:","const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\narr.splice(1, 2); // Deletes 2 elements starting from index 1\\nconsole.log(arr); // [1, 4, 5]\\n\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\nconst newArr = arr.filter(item => item !== 3); // Will delete every three\\n\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3];\\narr.splice(1, 0, \'a\', \'b\'); // Inserts \'a\', \'b\' at position 1\\nconsole.log(arr); // [1, \'a\', \'b\', 2, 3]\\n\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\narr.splice(1, 2, \'x\', \'y\'); // Will delete 2 and 3 and insert \'x\' and \'y\'  \\nconsole.log(arr); // [1, \'x\', \'y\', 4]"],[1082,"To intercept errors the following are used:\\n\\n- `try/catch` — for synchronous code.\\n\\n- `window.onerror` or `addEventListener(\'error\')` — for global errors.\\n\\n- `Promise.catch()` — for asynchronous code (`fetch`, for example).","- `try/catch` (Synchronous errors)\\n\\n- The global interception (`window.onerror`)\\n\\n- The handling of asynchronous errors (`unhandledrejection`)","try {\\n    // The code that may raise an error\\n    const data = JSON.parse(\'invalid JSON\');\\n} catch (error) {\\n    console.error(\'Error:\', error.message);  \\n}\\n\\nwindow.onerror = function(message, url, line, col, error) {\\n  console.log(`Error: ${message} at ${url}:${line}:${col}`);\\n  return true; // Cancels the standard error message  \\n};\\n\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'unhandledrejection\', (event) => {\\n  console.error(\'An unhandled error in a Promise:\', event.reason);  \\n});  \\nfetch(\'non-existent-url\').catch(error => {\\n  console.log(\'A fetch error:\', error);  \\n});"],[1083,"- `throw \'message\'` throws a string, but without a call stack and without the details of the error.\\n\\n- `throw new Error(\'message\')` creates an error object with `stack`, `name` and other properties, which simplifies debugging.","- `throw \'message\'` (A primitive)\\n\\nThe problems:\\n\\n- There is no call stack (`error.stack`).\\n\\n- There are no standard properties (`name`, `message`).\\n\\n- It is harder to analyse in the logs.\\n\\n- ` throw new Error(\'message\')` (An error object)\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- There is a `name` (`Error` or `TypeError`, for example).\\n\\n- There is a `stack` — you can see where the error took place.\\n\\n- You can create custom error classes (`class MyError extends Error`).\\n\\nWhen should you use which?\\n\\n- `new Error()` is always better if you need to debug.\\n\\n- `throw \'message\'` can be used for simple cases, but it is not recommended.\\n\\nAn example of a custom error:","try {\\n  throw \'An error has taken place\';  \\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.log(error); // \\"An error has taken place\\" (just a string)  \\n}\\n\\ntry {\\n  throw new Error(\'A serious error\');  \\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.log(error.message); // \\"A serious error\\"  \\n  console.log(error.stack); // The call stack for debugging  \\n}\\n\\nclass ValidationError extends Error {\\n  constructor(message) {\\n    super(message);  \\n    this.name = \'ValidationError\';  \\n  }  \\n}  \\nthrow new ValidationError(\'Invalid data\');"],[1084,"You can use `position: fixed` + JavaScript for the dynamic computation of an element\'s position on scrolling. You can also apply an `IntersectionObserver` to track an element\'s position and switch its styles.","- The use of `position: fixed` + JavaScript\\n\\nIf `sticky` is not available, its behaviour can be imitated:\\n\\n- On the scrolling of the page, compute when the element has to \\"stick\\".\\n\\n- Switch `position: relative` to `position: fixed` when the boundary is reached.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- The use of an `IntersectionObserver`\\n\\nA more modern way is to track when an element reaches the boundary:\\n\\n- An alternative with `transform: translateY`\\n\\nIf you need smooth behaviour, you can animate the element\'s position:","const stickyElement = document.querySelector(\'.sticky\');\\nconst offset = stickyElement.offsetTop;\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\\n  if (window.scrollY >= offset) {\\n    stickyElement.style.position = \'fixed\';\\n    stickyElement.style.top = \'0\';\\n  } else {\\n    stickyElement.style.position = \'relative\';\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver(\\n    (entries) => {\\n        entries.forEach((entry) => {\\n            if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n              entry.target.style.position = \'fixed\';\\n              entry.target.style.top = \'0\';\\n            } else {\\n              entry.target.style.position = \'relative\';\\n            }\\n        });\\n    },\\n    { threshold: 1 }\\n);\\n\\nobserver.observe(document.querySelector(\'.sticky\'));\\n\\n.sticky {\\n  position: relative;\\n  transition: transform 0.2s;\\n}\\n.sticky.fixed {\\n  transform: translateY(var(--scroll-offset));\\n}\\n\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\\n  const scrollY = window.scrollY;\\n  stickyElement.classList.toggle(\'fixed\', scrollY > offset);\\n});"],[1085,"A Service Worker is a script working in the background, separately from the web page. It lets you:\\n\\n- Cache resources for offline working.\\n\\n- Intercept network requests (for a PWA, for example).\\n\\n- Send push notifications.","The main capabilities:\\n\\n- Offline access\\nA Service Worker can save files in a cache and hand them over when there is no internet.\\n\\n- The management of requests\\nYou can intercept `fetch` requests and decide whether to take the data from the network or from the cache.\\n\\n- Background processes\\nIt works even when the site is closed (for push notifications, for example).\\n\\nAn example of the registration of a Service Worker:\\n\\nAn example of caching in `sw.js`:","// The main code (main.js)\\nif (\'serviceWorker\' in navigator) {\\n    navigator.serviceWorker.register(\'/sw.js\')\\n        .then(() => console.log(\'The SW has been registered\'))\\n        .catch(err => console.log(\'Error:\', err));\\n}\\n\\nconst CACHE_NAME = \'my-cache-v1\';\\nconst urlsToCache = [\'/\', \'/styles.css\', \'/script.js\'];\\n\\nself.addEventListener(\'install\', (event) => {\\n    event.waitUntil(\\n        caches.open(CACHE_NAME)\\n            .then(cache => cache.addAll(urlsToCache))\\n     );\\n});\\n\\nself.addEventListener(\'fetch\', (event) => {\\n    event.respondWith(\\n        caches.match(event.request)\\n            .then(response => response || fetch(event.request))\\n    );\\n});"],[1086,"To cancel a `fetch` an `AbortController` is used. We create the controller, pass it into `fetch` and call `abort()` when it is needed.","1. The use of an `AbortController`\\n\\n2. The integration with React/Axios\\nIn Axios there is a built-in cancellation through a `CancelToken` (in the older versions) or an `AbortController` (the newer versions).\\nAn example with Axios:\\n\\n3. Why is this needed?\\n\\n- If the user has left the page but the request is still being carried out.\\n\\n- On frequent changes of filters (a search with autocomplete, for example).\\n\\n- For the optimisation of performance.\\n\\nImportant: After `abort()` the Promise passes into the `rejected` state with an `AbortError` error.","const controller = new AbortController();\\nconst signal = controller.signal;\\n\\nfetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', { signal })\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .catch(err => {\\n        if (err.name === \'AbortError\') {\\n            console.log(\'The request has been cancelled\');\\n        } else {\\n            console.error(\'Error:\', err);\\n        }\\n  });\\n\\n// We cancel the request after 2 seconds\\nsetTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 2000);\\n\\nconst controller = new AbortController();\\n\\naxios.get(\'https://api.example.com/data\', {\\n    signal: controller.signal\\n})\\n  .then(response => console.log(response.data))\\n  .catch(err => {\\n      if (axios.isCancel(err)) {\\n          console.log(\'The request has been cancelled\');\\n      }\\n  });\\n\\n// The cancellation\\ncontroller.abort();"],[1087,"To receive data in real time the following are used:\\n\\n- WebSocket — a two-way connection between the client and the server.\\n\\n- Server-Sent Events (SSE) — a stream of one-way messages from the server.\\n\\n- Long Polling — requests with a long wait for the response.","1. WebSocket\\nThe ws:// or wss:// (secure) protocol, which supports two-way communication.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- The instantaneous transfer of data.\\n\\n- Support for binary data.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- It is harder to configure on the server.\\n\\n2. Server-Sent Events (SSE)\\nA one-way stream of data from the server (for notifications, for example).\\nThe client-side code:\\n\\nThe server (Node.js + Express):\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- Simplicity of implementation.\\n\\n- Automatic reconnection.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- Server to client only.\\n\\n3. Long Polling\\nThe client sends a request, and the server answers only when there is data.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- It works even without WebSocket.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- A high load on the server.","const socket = new WebSocket(\'wss://example.com/ws\');\\n\\nsocket.onopen = () => console.log(\'The connection has been established\');  \\nsocket.onmessage = (event) => console.log(\'Received:\', event.data);  \\nsocket.onclose = () => console.log(\'The connection has been closed\');  \\n\\n// The sending of data  \\nsocket.send(\'Hello, server!\');\\n\\nconst eventSource = new EventSource(\'/updates\');  \\n\\neventSource.onmessage = (event) => {  \\n    console.log(\'A new message:\', event.data);  \\n};\\n\\napp.get(\'/updates\', (req, res) => {  \\n    res.setHeader(\'Content-Type\', \'text/event-stream\');  \\n    setInterval(() => {  \\n        res.write(`data: ${new Date().toISOString()}\\\\n\\\\n`);  \\n   }, 1000);  \\n});\\n\\nfunction fetchUpdates() {  \\n    fetch(\'/poll\')  \\n        .then(response => response.json())  \\n        .then(data => {  \\n            console.log(\'The data:\', data);  \\n            fetchUpdates(); // A new request  \\n        });  \\n}"],[1088,"A Promise is an object that represents the result of an asynchronous operation. It can be in one of three states: waiting (`pending`), success (`fulfilled`) or error (`rejected`). Promises help you avoid \\"callback hell\\" and make code cleaner.","A Promise is a mechanism in JavaScript for working with asynchronous operations such as HTTP requests, the reading of files or timers. Instead of nested callbacks (`callback hell`), promises let you write more comprehensible and linear code.\\nA promise has three states:\\n\\n- Pending — the operation has not yet finished.\\n\\n- Fulfilled — the operation has been carried out successfully.\\n\\n- Rejected — an error has taken place.\\n\\nAn example of the creation and the use of a promise:\\n\\nPromises can be combined into chains (`then().then()...`), their errors handled through `.catch()` and several operations carried out in parallel with `Promise.all()`.","const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    const success = true;\\n    if (success) {\\n      resolve(\\"The data has been received successfully!\\");\\n    } else {\\n      reject(\\"An error in the loading of the data.\\");\\n    }\\n  }, 1000);\\n});\\n\\nmyPromise\\n  .then((result) => console.log(result)) // Success\\n  .catch((error) => console.error(error)); // An error"],[1089,"In JavaScript there are 4 main ways of creating an object:\\n\\n- An object literal (`{}`) — the simplest way, suitable for one-off objects.\\n\\n- A constructor (`new Object()`) — the analogue of a literal, but hardly used.\\n\\n- A constructor function or a class (`new MyClass()`) — for a multitude of objects of one and the same kind with methods.\\n\\n- `Object.create()` — creates an object with the prototype specified (flexible, but harder).\\n\\nThe main differences:\\n\\n- Literals and `new Object()` are suitable for simple cases.\\n\\n- Classes and constructors are for OOP and repeating structures.\\n\\n- `Object.create()` is for precise control of the prototypes.","1. An object literal (`{}`)\\nThe simplest and most popular way. It is used for the creation of one-off objects — configs or data, for example.\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- Simplicity and readability.\\n\\n- It requires no additional code.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- It is not suitable for the creation of a multitude of objects of one and the same kind.\\n\\n2. A constructor (`new Object()`)\\nAn alternative to a literal, but practically unused, since it gives no advantages.\\n\\nThe difference from `{}`:\\n\\n- A literal is shorter and more convenient.\\n\\n- In modern projects `new Object()` is hardly ever met with.\\n\\n3. A constructor function or a class\\nIt is suitable when you have to create many objects with the same structure and methods.\\nAn example with a constructor function (the old style):\\n\\nAn example with a class (the modern style):\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- It is convenient for OOP and inheritance.\\n\\n- The code is easier to scale.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- It is excessive for simple objects.\\n\\n4. `Object.create()`\\nIt creates an object with the prototype specified. It is useful for the fine tuning of the chain of inheritance.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- You can create an object without a prototype (`Object.create(null)`).\\n\\n- It lets you add properties through descriptors (`enumerable: false`, for example).\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\n\\n- For advanced inheritance without classes.\\n\\n- For the creation of \\"clean\\" objects (for dictionaries, for example).","const user = {\\n  name: \\"Alex\\",\\n  age: 25,\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nconst car = new Object();\\ncar.model = \\"Tesla\\";\\ncar.year = 2023;\\n\\nfunction Person(name, age) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.age = age;\\n  this.sayHello = function() {\\n    console.log(`Hi, I\'m ${this.name}!`);\\n  };\\n}\\nconst person1 = new Person(\\"Anna\\", 30);\\n\\nclass Person {\\n  constructor(name, age) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n    this.age = age;\\n  }\\n  sayHello() {\\n    console.log(`Hi, I\'m ${this.name}!`);\\n  }\\n}\\nconst person2 = new Person(\\"John\\", 25);\\n\\nconst animal = { eats: true };\\nconst rabbit = Object.create(animal); // rabbit.__proto__ === animal\\nrabbit.jumps = true;\\n\\nconsole.log(rabbit.eats); // true (the property from the prototype)"],[1090,"An object holds data in the form `{ key: value }` without a guarantee of the order. An array is an ordered list of values with numeric indexes (`[0, 1, 2]`).\\n\\n- An object is suitable for the description of entities (a `user` with the fields `name` and `age`, for example).\\n\\n- An array is used for lists (`[1, 2, 3]`, for example), in which the order matters.","An object is an unordered collection of properties, in which every value is available by a key (a string or a symbol).\\n\\nAn array is an ordered collection with the automatic numbering of the elements (the indexes begin at `0`).\\n\\nThe key differences:\\n\\n- The structure:\\n\\n- An object: `{ key: value }` (`user.name`, for example).\\n\\n- An array: `[element1, element2]` (`fruits[0]`, for example).\\n\\n- The order of the elements:\\n\\n- In an array the order is fixed (it matters for the `sort` and `map` operations).\\n\\n- In an object the order is not guaranteed (but since ES6 the order of the addition is preserved).\\n\\n- The methods:\\n\\n- Arrays have special methods (`push`, `pop`, `map`).\\n\\n- Objects do not (but you can use `Object.keys()` and `Object.values()`).\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- An object: For data with named properties (configuration or entities, for example).\\n\\n- An array: For lists in which the order matters or in which you need the iteration methods (`forEach`, `filter`).","const user = { \\n  name: \\"Alex\\",  // The key \\"name\\", the value \\"Alex\\"\\n  age: 30        // The key \\"age\\", the value 30\\n};\\nconsole.log(user.name); // \\"Alex\\" (access by the key)\\n\\nconst fruits = [\\"apple\\", \\"banana\\"];\\nconsole.log(fruits[0]); // \\"apple\\" (access by the index)"],[1091,"- `event.target` is the element that raised the event (a button inside a div that was clicked on, for example).\\n\\n- `event.currentTarget` is the element to which the handler is attached (the div itself, for example).","An example of the HTML:\\n\\nThe JavaScript:\\n\\nThe difference:\\n\\n- If you click on the button:\\n\\n- `event.target` is `button#child` (where the click landed).\\n\\n- `event.currentTarget` is `div#parent` (where the handler hangs).\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- `event.target` — to find out on which element the event took place (for the delegation of events, for example).\\n\\n- `event.currentTarget` — to obtain the element that is handling the event (for access to its properties, for example).","<div id=\\"parent\\">\\n  <button id=\\"child\\">Click me</button>\\n</div>\\n\\ndocument.getElementById(\\"parent\\").addEventListener(\\"click\\", function(event) {\\n  console.log(\\"target:\\", event.target.id);      // \\"child\\" (the source of the event)\\n  console.log(\\"currentTarget:\\", event.currentTarget.id); // \\"parent\\" (the handler)\\n});"],[1092,"- `stopPropagation()` stops the bubbling of the event (but the other handlers on that same element will fire).\\n\\n- `stopImmediatePropagation()` stops the bubbling and all of the remaining handlers on that element.","An example:\\n\\nWith `stopImmediatePropagation()`:\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- `stopPropagation()` — when you have to forbid the bubbling but give the other handlers on that element a chance.\\n\\n- `stopImmediatePropagation()` — when you have to cancel every subsequent handler completely (for high-priority events, for example).\\n\\nImportant: Neither of the methods cancels the browser\'s default action (for that there is `event.preventDefault()`).","const div = document.querySelector(\\"div\\");\\n\\ndiv.addEventListener(\\"click\\", (event) => {\\n  console.log(\\"The first handler\\");\\n  event.stopPropagation(); // Will stop the bubbling, but the second handler will fire\\n});\\n\\ndiv.addEventListener(\\"click\\", (event) => {\\n  console.log(\\"The second handler\\"); // Will be carried out all the same\\n});\\n\\ndocument.body.addEventListener(\\"click\\", () => {\\n  console.log(\\"Will not fire (the bubbling has been stopped)\\");\\n});\\n\\ndiv.addEventListener(\\"click\\", (event) => {\\n  console.log(\\"The first handler\\");\\n  event.stopImmediatePropagation(); // Will stop everything\\n});\\n\\ndiv.addEventListener(\\"click\\", () => {\\n  console.log(\\"This code will not be carried out\\");\\n});"],[1093,"To search for every element on a page the following are used:\\n\\n- `document.querySelectorAll(\'*\')` — returns every element (node) in the form of a collection.\\n\\n- `document.getElementsByTagName(\'*\')` — an old method, which works analogously but returns a \\"live\\" collection.\\n\\nBoth methods find every element, `<html>`, `<head>`, `<body>` and their contents included.","1. `document.querySelectorAll(\'*\')`\\nThe modern method, which supports CSS selectors. It returns a static collection (it is not updated when the DOM changes).\\n\\n2. `document.getElementsByTagName(\'*\')`\\nIt works in every browser but returns a \\"live\\" collection (it changes when the DOM is updated). It is less convenient for complex selections.\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- `querySelectorAll` is slower on large pages but more flexible.\\n\\n- `getElementsByTagName` is faster but does not support complex selectors (`\'.class\'`, for example).\\n\\nAn example of the use:","const allElements = document.querySelectorAll(\'*\');\\nconsole.log(allElements.length); // The number of elements on the page\\n\\nconst allLiveElements = document.getElementsByTagName(\'*\');\\nconsole.log(allLiveElements.length); // Every element too\\n\\n// Find every element with the class \\"button\\"\\nconst buttons = document.querySelectorAll(\'.button\');\\n\\n// Find every div on the page\\nconst divs = document.getElementsByTagName(\'div\');"],[1094,"A `number` is a primitive type; it is held on the stack (fast access, a fixed size). An `object` is a reference type; its data is held in the heap (the dynamic allocation of memory), while only the reference to it is saved on the stack.","In JavaScript the primitive types (`number`, `string` and `boolean`, for example) are held on the stack — an area of memory with fast access and a fixed size. When you create a variable with a number, its value is written straight onto the stack.\\nObjects (`object`, `array`, `function`) are held in the heap — a dynamic area of memory. On the stack only the reference (the address in memory) is saved, pointing to the place in the heap where the object\'s data lies.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThis explains why primitives are copied by value and objects by reference.","let a = 5; // a number (held on the stack)\\nlet b = a; // the value is copied (5)\\nb = 10;    // the copy is changed, \'a\' remains 5\\n\\nlet obj1 = { value: 5 }; // an object (the data in the heap, the reference on the stack)\\nlet obj2 = obj1;         // the reference is copied, both point to one object\\nobj2.value = 10;         // the shared object is changed, obj1.value will also become 10"],[1095,"JavaScript is single-threaded: it carries out one operation at a time. By means of asynchronous mechanisms (`Promise` and `setTimeout`, for example) and Web Workers, however, parallel tasks can be imitated.","JavaScript works in one thread (the main thread — the UI thread), which means that at one moment in time only one operation is carried out. This prevents problems with race conditions, but it can lead to \\"freezes\\" if the code is carried out for a long time.\\nThe browser APIs (`fetch` and `setTimeout`, for example) and the event loop, however, let asynchronous operations be handled without the blocking of the thread. For example:\\n\\nFor genuine multithreading there are Web Workers — they run code in background threads, but they have no access to the DOM.","console.log(\\"Start\\");\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"Timeout\\"), 0);\\nconsole.log(\\"End\\");\\n// The output: Start -> End -> Timeout"],[1096,"Yes, by means of Web Workers. They let you run JavaScript in background threads, but they cannot change the DOM and they have limited access to the browser\'s API.","Web Workers are a mechanism for carrying code out in separate threads. They are useful for heavy computations (the handling of data or mathematical calculations, for example) so as not to block the main thread.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe limitations:\\n\\n- Workers have no access to `document` or `window`.\\n\\n- The communication between the threads takes place through `postMessage` (the copying of the data rather than shared memory).\\n\\n- For more complex scenarios you can use a `SharedArrayBuffer`, but that requires care because of the risk of race conditions.\\n\\nMultithreading in a browser is therefore possible, but with limitations.","// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker(\\"worker.js\\");\\nworker.postMessage(\\"Start\\"); // The sending of a message to the worker\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => console.log(e.data); // The receiving of the response\\n\\n// worker.js\\nonmessage = (e) => {\\n  console.log(\\"Worker received:\\", e.data);\\n  postMessage(\\"Done\\"); // The sending of the result back\\n};"],[1097,"The main thread (the UI thread) is responsible for the execution of JavaScript, the painting of the page and the handling of events. A Web Worker is a background thread, which can carry out complex computations but has no access to the DOM and to some of the browser\'s APIs.","The main thread in a browser:\\n\\n- It manages the execution of JavaScript, the painting of the page (the rendering) and the handling of the user\'s events (clicks, input and so on).\\n\\n- If a long synchronous operation is carried out in it (a loop of a billion iterations, for example), the interface will \\"freeze\\".\\n\\nA Web Worker:\\n\\n- It works in a separate thread without blocking the main one.\\n\\n- It has no access to the DOM, `window`, `document` and many APIs (`localStorage`, for example).\\n\\n- It communicates with the main thread through `postMessage` (the asynchronous exchange of data).\\n\\n- It is useful for heavy computations (the handling of large arrays or machine learning in the browser, for example).\\n\\nAn example:","// The main thread (main.js)\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\');\\nworker.postMessage({ data: [1, 2, 3] }); // The sending of the data\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => console.log(\'Result:\', e.data); // The receiving of the result\\n\\n// The Web Worker (worker.js)\\nonmessage = (e) => {\\n  const result = e.data.data.map(x => x * 2); // The computations in the background\\n  postMessage(result); // The sending back\\n};"],[1098,"The main thread and a Web Worker communicate through asynchronous messages by means of `postMessage` and the `onmessage` event. The data is passed by copying (not by shared memory) unless a `SharedArrayBuffer` is used.","The mechanism of the interaction:\\n\\n- `postMessage(data)` — the sending of data from one thread to another.\\n\\n- `onmessage` — the handler for receiving the data.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- The data is passed by copying (it is serialised through structured cloning).\\n\\n- To pass large volumes of data without copying you can use a `SharedArrayBuffer` (but that requires synchronisation through `Atomics`).\\n\\n- The messages are asynchronous: the sending through `postMessage` does not block the thread.","// The main thread\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\');\\nworker.postMessage({ command: \'sum\', numbers: [1, 2, 3] });\\n\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {\\n  console.log(\'Sum:\', e.data); // Will receive 6\\n};\\n\\n// The Web Worker (worker.js)\\nonmessage = (e) => {\\n  if (e.data.command === \'sum\') {\\n    const sum = e.data.numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\\n    postMessage(sum);\\n  }\\n};"],[1099,"Jest uses `jsdom` to emulate the browser environment in Node.js. This means that tests can work with DOM methods without running a real browser, but not every browser API is fully supported, and the behaviour can differ a little.","Jest is a test runner, which by default works in the Node.js environment. In order to test code that depends on the DOM, it uses the `jsdom` library.\\nHow `jsdom` works:\\n\\n- The emulation of the DOM\\nIt creates a `document` and a `window` object and other global variables similar to the browser\'s.\\n\\n- The execution in Node.js\\nThe tests are run in Node.js, which makes them fast and requires no browser.\\n\\n- The limitations\\nNot every API is implemented; WebGL or complex animations, for example, may work differently.\\n\\nThe difference from a browser:\\n\\n- The performance is higher (there is no graphical render).\\n\\n- Limited support for APIs — jsdom does not fully reproduce a browser.\\n\\n- There is no real painting — the tests check the structure of the DOM rather than the visual display.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nJest with `jsdom` is excellently suited for testing the logic and the structure of a UI without the costs of a real browser, but for testing the rendering it is better to use e2e tools.",null],[1100,"`MutationObserver` carries its callbacks out in microtasks, so that changes in the DOM are handled right after the current task but before the rendering and the macrotasks. This lets you react to changes fast while preserving high performance.","`MutationObserver` is an API for tracking changes in the DOM. Its callbacks are carried out in microtasks.\\nThe reasons for the choice of microtasks:\\n\\n- The priority of the execution\\nMicrotasks are carried out after the current task but before the next paint, which ensures a minimal delay in the reaction.\\n\\n- The grouping of changes\\nSeveral changes in the DOM can be combined into a single call of the callback.\\n\\n- Synchronicity with respect to the rendering\\nIt lets you react before the browser repaints the page.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe use of microtasks makes `MutationObserver` a fast and efficient tool for tracking the DOM without blocking the user interface.","const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {\\n  console.log(\'A change in the DOM\');\\n});\\n\\nobserver.observe(document.body, { childList: true });\\n\\ndocument.body.appendChild(document.createElement(\'div\'));\\n// The callback will be called in a microtask"]]');export{e as default};